1804:
** ἐξαγγ-έλλω**,
**tell out, proclaim, make known,** frequently with collateral sense of **betraying** a secret, εἰ μὴ μητρυιὴ.. Ἑρμέᾳ ἐξήγγειλεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.390; εἰσὶ γάρ, εἰσὶν οἱ πάντ᾽ ἐξαγγέλλοντες ἐκείνῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.18, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.27, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.9, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.5; ἐ. τινὶ ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33; ἐ. τινὶ προσιὸν τὸ στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.10; τινὶ οὕνεκα.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1393; τινὶ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 601d}}; τινὶ τὰ περί τι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 359e; ἐ. κατά τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1313b34}}; of traitors and deserters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.42, etc.}}; compare {ἐξάγγελος}:—_middle_, **cause to be proclaimed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.95, 6.10, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 148}}: with _infinitive_, **promise to** do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 531}}:—_passive_, **to be reported,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.β᾽, +others}}; ἐξηγγέλθη βασιλεὺς ἀθροίζων the king **was reported to be** collecting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.6: _impersonal_, ἐξαγγέλλεται}} **it is reported,** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 3.2.18; πολιορκεῖσθαι τοὺς.. στρατιώτας ἐξηγγέλλετο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.162.}}
**II** **express,** ἔννοιαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.5}}:—_passive_, ἐ. λέξει **to be expressed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1460b11; ὀνόμασι 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 102e.}}
**III** **narrate,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 15.184b.}}

1806:
** ἐξάγω**,
_perfect_ **-ῆχα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.19}}, **-αγήγοχα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.34.10 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**lead out, lead away**:
**I** of persons, mostly with _genitive_ loci, μεγάροιο, πόγηος, ὁμίλου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.458, 23.372, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.353; μάχης [prev. work] 35: with ἐκ.., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.106, 20.21; ἐ. ἐκ τῆς χώρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.148, +others}}; Ἄργεος ἐξαγαγόντες **having brought** her **out from** Argos, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.379}}; **bring out of** prison, **release,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.34.4, +others (3rd c.BC), NT.Act.16.39}}; **bring forth into the world,** τόν γε.. Εἰλείθυια ἐξάγαγε πρὸ φόωσδε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.188; νεοττούς}}**lead out of the nest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 613b12; ἐ. Λυδοὺς ἐς μάχην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79, etc.; ἐπὶ θήραν τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.14}}; **lead out** to execution, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.10, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, με τήνδε τὴν ὁδὸν.. ἐξήγαγε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 98.}}
**I.b** seemingly _intransitive_, **march out** (i.e. στρατόν), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.14, 5.4.38, etc.}}: generally, **go out,** ὡς εἰς θήραν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.4.18}}; εἰς προνομάς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.1.24: once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τύμβον.. ἕνα χεύομεν ἐξαγαγόντες}} let us **go out** and pile one tomb for all, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.336 (3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus)}}; also, **come to an end,** οἱ μεγάλοι πόνοι συντόμως ἐ. soon **pass away,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 447, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.33.}}
**I.2** **draw out from, release from,** ἀχέων τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.51}}; ἐ. τινὰ ἐκ τοῦ ζῆν, i.e. put him to death, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.16.13}}; ἑαυτὸν ἐκ τοῦ ζῆν commit suicide, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 38.16.5; τοῦ ζῆν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1076b; τοῦ σώματος [prev. author] “Comp. Demetr.Ant.” 6; simply ἐ. ἑαυτόν 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.188, compare 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 5.29; ὅταν ἡμᾶς τὸ χρεὼν ἐξάγῃ 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus 49.}}
**I.3** **eject** a claimant from property {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (ἐξαγωγή 11), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.4, 32.17, 44.32, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be turned out,** ὑπὸ τοῦ παιδοτρίβου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Socraticus Philosophus 37.}}
**II** of merchandise, etc., **carry out, export,** ῥῶπον χθονός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 263, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 278, 282, etc.}}; εἴ τις ἐξαγαγὼν παῖδα ληφθείη **exporting** him as a slave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.10, compare 13.67}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.2, etc.; τὰ -όμενα}}**exports,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359b22; οὔτε γὰρ ἐξήγετο.. οὐδὲν οὔτ᾽ εἰσήγετο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.145}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.3.}}
**II.2** **draw off** water, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Oec.” 20.12 (_passive_), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.17}}; **draw out,** of perspiration, ὑπὸ τοῦ ἡλίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 8 (_passive_)}}; so, **carry off** by purgative medicines, ἕλμινθας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Geoponica” 12.26.1, compare 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.152.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.134c, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.5}}: generally, **get rid of,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.6.3.}}
**II.3** of building, **draw** or **carry farther out,** αἱμασιάν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.22}}:—_passive_, ὁ περίβολος πανταχῇ ἐξήχθη τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93.}}
**II.4** of expenses, ἐπὶ πλεῖστον ἐξάγεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.25.}}
**III** **bring forth, produce,** οὐκ ἐξάγουσι καρπὸν οἱ ψευδεῖς λόγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 834; ᾠά}}**hatch,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 564b8}}; **call forth, excite,** δάκρυ τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 770}}:—_middle_, γέλωτα ἐξαγαγέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.15; μικρὰ ἆθλα πολλοὺς πόνους ἐξάγεται}}**elicit, induce,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hier.” 9.11.}}
**IV** **lead on, carry away, excite,** τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1080, “Supp.” 79; τινὰ ἐπ᾽ οἶκτον [prev. author] “Ion” 361, compare “HF” 1212 (_anapaest meter_); ἐς τοὺς κινδύνους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45}}; in bad sense, **lead on, tempt,** οὐδέ με οἶνος ἐ. ὥστε εἰπεῖν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 414; ἐ. ἐπὶ τὰ πονηρότερα τὸν ὄχλον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.89}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 775 (Lyric poetry); εἰς τὸ διδόναι λόγον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.922f}}:—_passive_, **to be led on to** do a thing, c._infinitive_, ἐξήχθην ὀλοφύρασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.61; ταῦτα.. ἐξήχθημεν εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 572b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.21; ἃ μὲν ἄν τις ἐξαχθῇ πρᾶξαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.41, compare 74; εἰς ἅμιλλαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 29}}: _absolutely_, **to be carried away** by passion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.15; ὑπὸ τοῦ θυμοῦ 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.17.8, etc.}}; ἐξάγουσα ὀδύνη **distracting** pain, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus}} _middle_ cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.8.1.}}
**IV.2** **lead away,** [λόγον] εἰς ἄλλας ὑποθέσεις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.42e; προβλήματα ἐ. εἰς ὀργανικὰς κατασκευάς}}**reduce,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Marc.” 14 (also εἰς ἔργον πρόβλημα [prev. passage])}}; ἐ. εἰς τὸ ἀνώτερον, Latin _altius repetere,_ {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.639e}}; πρὸς τὴν Ἑλληνικὴν διάλεκτον ἐξάγειν τοὔνομα **express** in Greek, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Num.” 13.}}
**V** **exercise,** τὴν ἀρχὴν οὐκέτι βασιλικῶς, ἀλλὰ τυραννικώτερον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.56, compare “IG” 22.1304.4, 14}}; **carry out** instructions, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Michel” 409.18 (Naxos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**VI** **give directions** in a will, ἐμαυτὸν οὕτως ἐξάγω Lyconap.{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.72.}}
**VII** _intransitive_, **pass one's life,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.43.}}

1807:
** ἐξαιρ-έω**,
_future_ -ησω, later ἐξελῶ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.56, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ ἐξεῖλον, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry ἔξελον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.56, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.26}}; _infinitive_ ἐξελεῖν:—_middle_, _future_ ἐξαιρήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 924; later ἐξελοῦμαι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.9:}}_aorist 2_ ἐξειλόμην,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|rarely 1 ἐξῃρησάμην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 761 (perhaps interpolated):}} —_passive_, _perfect_ -ῄρημαι, _Ionic dialect_ **-αραίρημαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}:—**take out,** ἔνθεν.. ἔξελε πέπλους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.229}}; ἐπείνιν καθαροῦ λέβητος ἔξελε Κλωθώ Pi.[same place]; τὸ δέλτα τοῦ ὀνόματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 413e}}; simply, **take out,** τὴν κοιλίην, τὴν νηδύν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40 (tmesis), 87; πρὶν ἀνταράξας πῖαρ ἐξεῖλεν γάλα 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 36.21}}:—_passive_, εἰ τὸ ἔαρ ἐκ τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ ἐξαραιρημένον εἴη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.162, cf. Pericl. cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1365a33.}}
**2** _middle_, **take out for oneself,** φαρέτρης ἐξείλετο πικρὸν ὀϊστόν from **his** quiver, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.323}}; ἐξελέσθαι τὰ μεγάλα ἱστία **their** large sails, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.13}}; ἐ. τὰ φορτία **discharge their** cargoes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.196; τὰ ἀγώγιμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.16; τὸν σῖτον ἐς [τὴν στοὰν] ἐξαιρεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.90: _absolutely_, Syngr. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.13, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be discharged,** of a cargo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.8.}}
**II** **take from** a common stock, **reserve,** κούρην, ἣν ἄρα μοι γέρας ἔξελον υἷες Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.56; Ἀλκινόῳ δ᾽ αὐτὴν γέρας ἔξελον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.10, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.627; βασιλέϊ τεμένεα ἐξελὼν καὶ ἱερωσύνας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.161; Νίσῳ ἐ. χθόνα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 24.5; θεοῖσιν ἀκροθίνια 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 470; κλήρους τοῖς θεοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.50}}:—_middle_, **choose for oneself, carry off as booty,** τὴν ἐκ Αυρνησσοῦ ἐξείλετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.690, compare 9.130}}; **choose,** μενοεικέα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.232; μίαν ἕκαστος σιτοποιὸν ἐ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.150, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.20; ταύτας ἐξείλεθ᾽ αὑτῷ κτῆμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 245}}; δῶρον.. πόλεος ἐξελέσθαι **to have accepted** as a gift, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 541 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, **to be given as a special honour,** τινί to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.114}}; ἐξαραιρημένος Ποσειδέωνι **dedicated** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.148; γέρεα.. σφι ἦν τάδε ἐξαραιρημένα [prev. author] 2.168}}; ἐ. αὐτοῖς **set apart** for them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 117c; τὰ τεμένη τὰ ἐξῃρημένα “IG” 12.45.10}}; of funds, **to be set apart, ear-marked**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 577.64 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; but τοῦ ἀργυρίου τοῦ ἐκ τοῦ λιθοτομείου ἐξαιρουμένου moneys **received** from.., {{Refs|“IG” 22.47.}}
**II.2** **take out of** a number, **except,** μητέρας ἐξελόντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.150; Σιμμίαν ἐξαιρῶ λόγου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.15.}}
**III** **remove** people from their country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30; τοὺς ἐν τῇ λίμνῃ κατοικημένους [prev. author] 5.16}}; στρουθούς (i.e. ἐκ τοῦ νηοῦ) {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.159}}: generally, **remove,** τὸν λίθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.125; ἐκ τοῦ λυχνούχου τὸν λύχνον 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 102; πατρὸς φόβον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 991, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.23}}; ὀδυρμούς, ἄγνοιαν, ἔρωτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 387d, “Lg.” 771e, “Smp.” 186d; ἀλλήλων τὴν ἀπιστίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.4}}:—_middle_, νεῖκος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 904}}; ὑμῶν ἐ. τὴν διαβολὴν.. ταύτην **remove** this prejudice from your minds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19a, compare 24a.}}
**III.2** **get rid of,** [ὗν] ἐκ τῆς χώρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36; θῆρας χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 18}}; **make away with,** παῖδας, θῆρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HF” 39, 154; Ἀθηναίους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.19.}}
**III.2.b** **destroy,** πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.113, 4.69, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.30; χωρία [prev. author] 23.115; οἰκίδιον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 199, compare 278; φρούριον 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.86.}}
**III.2.c** **annul, bring to naught,** θέσφατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 908 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.36.}}
**III.3** _middle_, ψυχήν, θυμόν, φρένας ἐξελέσθαι, either with _accusative_ _person_, **bereave** a person **of** life, etc., as μιν ἐξείλετο θυμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.460, 17.678 (so in Trag., 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 69, “IA” 972): or with _genitive_ _person_, as μευ φρένας ἐξέλετο Ζεύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.137, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 89; σεῦ ψυχὴν χαλκῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.754; μου τέρψιν ἐξείλου βίου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 347, etc.: rarely, with _dative_ _person_, Γλαύκῳ φρένας ἐξέλετο Ζεύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.234; [οἰωνοῖς] τέκνα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.218: in tmesi, ἐκ δέος εἵλετο γυίων 6.140; ἐκ θυμὸν ἕλοιο 20.62, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.381}}:—_middle_, **take away from one,** τὰ φίλτατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1208:}} —_passive_, ἐξαιρεθέντες τὸν Δημοκήδεα **having had** him **taken out of their hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137; τὸ ἐπιθυμοῦν τοῦ πλοῦ οὐκ ἐξῃρέθησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.24, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 519d, etc.}}
**III.4** _passive_, **to be removed from,** i.e. **transcend,** τοῦ τῶν ὄντων πλήθους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.546 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; ἑνάδες ἐξῃρημέναι **transcendent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]p.547 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 7; τὸ μᾶλλον -μένον μᾶλλον καὶ χωρεῖ διὰ τῶν ἄλλων [prev. work] 325.}} _adverb_ ἐξῃρημένως **transcendently,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 270}}; **ultimately,** opposed to προσεχῶς, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus in “de An.” 270.14.}}
**IV** _middle_, **set free, deliver,** τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 924, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 316}}; ἐκ τῶν κινδύνων τινά Decrees cited in {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.90; ἐκ τῆς ἀνάγκης “PPetr.” 3p.74; ἐκ τῶν θλίψεων NT.Act.7.10}}; ἐξαιρεῖσθαι εἰς ἐλευθερίαν **claim** as a freeman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.42, 10.14.}}
**IV.2** **bring to an end, accomplish,** πᾶν γὰρ ἐξαιρεῖ λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 516.}} —Freq. confounded with ἐξαίρω.

1808:
** ἐξαίρω**,
_Epic dialect_ **ἐξᾰείρω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below)}}, also in _Ionic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 21, compare ἀείρω, αἴρω}}: _aorist 1_ ἐξῆρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 358, etc.}}:—**lift up, lift off the earth,** ἐκ μὲν ἄμαξαν ἄειραν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.266; ἐκ δὲ κτήματ᾽ ἄειραν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.120 (elsewhere 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only _middle_, see below)}}; ἐξάρας [αὐτὸν] παίει ἐς τὴν γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.107}}; ἐ. χεῖρας in prayer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.62.8; κοῦφον ἐξάρας πόδα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 224}}; βάθρων ἐκ τῶνδέ μ᾽ ἐξάραντες **having bidden** me **rise** (from suppliant posture), {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 264, compare “Tr.” 1193}}; τίς σ᾽ ἐξῆρεν οἴκοθεν στόλο; **made** thee **start,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 358; ἡδοναῖς ἄμοχθον ἐ. βίον [prev. author] “Tr.” 147}}; ἐ. θώρακα **take** it **out** (of its case), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1133; πυρσόν 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 22.5:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 22.6.}}
**b** seemingly _intransitive_, **rise from the ground,** of a bird, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.50; ἐ. τῷ στρατεύματι}}**start,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.23.4, cf. LXX.Num.2.9.}}
**2** **raise in dignity, exalt, magnify,** Κλεισθένης [τὴν οἰκίην] ἐξῆρε (variant{-ήγειρε}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.126; ἐξάρας με ὑψοῦ καὶ τὴν πάτρην [prev. author] 9.79}}; ἄνω τὸ πρᾶγμα ἐ. **exaggerate** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.10; ἐπὶ μεῖζον ἐ. τὰ γενόμενα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.4; ὑψηλὸν ἐ. αὑτὸν ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494d; ἐ. ὑπόθεσιν 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.522S.}}; Rhet., **treat in elevated style,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.3; τὸν τῆς ἑρμηνείας τύπον ἐ. παρὰ τὸ εἰωθός 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.484S.; ἐπιστολαὶ μικρὸν ἐξηρμέναι 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 234}}; of music, ἐξηρμένον καὶ τεθαρρηκός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Heraclides Ponticus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.624d.}}
**3** **arouse, stir up,** θυμὸν ἐς ἀμπλακίην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 630; μηδὲν δεινὸν ἐξάρῃς μένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1066}}; ἐ. σε θανεῖν **excites** thy **wish** to die, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 322; ἐ. φρένα λακεῖν [prev. author] Alcaeus346; ἐ. χάριν χορείας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 981.}}
**4** **pervert,** λόγους δικαίων {{LXX|LXX.Deu.16.19.}}
**5** **remove,** ἔπιπλα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.177.21 (1st c.AD)}}; **make away with, get rid of,** ἐξάρατε τὸν πονηρὸν ἐξ ὑμῶν αὐτῶν {{NT|NT.1Cor.5.13}}:—_passive_, **to be carried away,** of a dam, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 133.19 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** _middle_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _3rd.pers._ _sing._ _aorist_ ἐξήρατο)}}, **carry off for oneself, earn,** δοιοὺς μισθούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.84; ὅσ᾽ ἂν οὐδέ ποτε ἐκ Τροίης ἐξήρατ᾽ Ὀδυσσεύς 5.39}}; ἐξάρατο ἕδνον **won** it as a dower, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.10; θοῶν ἐξήρατ᾽ ἀγώνων.. κειμήλια 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.122. (In 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus ἐξήρατο may have displaced ἐξήρετο, _aorist_ of ἐξάρνυμαι, see at {ἀείρω}.)}}]
**II.2** ἐξαίρεσθαι νόσον **take** a disease **on oneself, catch** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 491.}}
**II.3** **carry off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319c.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be raised,** [τὸ τεῖχος] ἐξῄρετο διπλήσιον τοῦ ἀρχαίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.133}}; **rise up, rise,** ἐξαιρόμενον νέφος οἰμωγῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 106; φλόξ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.5.1; κονιορτός [prev. author] 3.65.4.}}
**III.2** **swell,** uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 15}}; ἐξαειρόμενα (-εύμενα codices) ὑπὸ τῆς πιέξιος **swellings** caused by compression, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Fract.” 21.}}
**III.3** **to be excited, agitated,** ἐλπίδι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1461; ἐξαρθεὶς ὑπὸ μεγαλαυχίας}}**puffed up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 716a}}: with _participle_, ἐξήρθης κλύων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 109.}}
**III.4** ἐξηρμένος probably _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.4.5.}}

1809:
** ἐξαιτ-έω**,
**demand** or **ask for from** another, with _double_ _accusative_, τήνδε μ᾽ ἐξαιτεῖ χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 586, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1657, “Supp.” 120}}; ἐ. τινὰ πατρός **ask** her **in marriage** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 10}}; ἐ. τινά **demand the surrender of** a person, especially a criminal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.41 (_passive_), “IG” 22.457b17 (4th c.BC)}}; **demand** a slave for torture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.27, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.36; τὸν ἐλεύθερον ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.14 (also ἐ. τὴν βάσανον [prev. work]13)}}; ἐ. [τινὰ] βασανίζειν {{Refs|[prev. author] 37.51}}; σμικρὸν ἐ. **ask** or **beg for** little, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 5; ἐ. τινὰ ποιεῖν τι [prev. author] “OT” 1255, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 175.}}
**II** _middle_, **ask for oneself, demand,** _active_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.159, 9.87, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 656, etc.}}; χάριν παρά τινος{{NT|I.ys. 20.31; τινά NT.Luke.22.31}}; πέμψον τὸν δαίμονα ὃν ἐξῃτησάμην **for** whose **aid** I **prayed,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.434, compare 1290.}}
**II.2** in _middle_ also, ={παραιτοῦμαι}, **beg off, gain** his **pardon** or **release,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 662, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.3, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.15 (_passive_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 32, etc.; αὑτὸν ἐξαιτήσεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.99; also ἐ. ὑπέρ τινος}}**make intercession for..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 360: with}}_infinitive_, τοὺς κάτω.. ἐξῃτησάμην τύμβου κυρῆσαι I **begged** of them **to allow** me to obtain, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 49, compare “Med.” 971.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **avert by begging,** τὰ πρόσθεν σφάλματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Andr.” 54; τὰς γραφὰς παρανόμων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.196.}}

1811:
** ἐξᾰκολουθέω**,
**follow,** of persons, τοῖς φίλοις {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.10.7, cf. LXX.Job.31.9; μύθοις NT.2Pet.1.16, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJProoem.” 4.}}
**2** of things, **follow, result from,** with _dative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 181}}; **attend,** with _dative_, εὔνοια, φήμη ἐ. τινί, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.5.6, 5.78.4; ἔπαινοί τισι κατορθουμένοις 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 24}}; especially of penalties, ἐ. πρόστιμά τισι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 112v10 (2nd c.BC), “PTeb.” 5.132 (2nd c.BC)}}; also of obligations, **fall on** one, {{Refs|“CPR” 5.15, etc.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **follow, result,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 58.5, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.15.4}}; also of logical consequences, πάντα ταῦτα ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.22.16, cf. Polystr. p.5 W.}}

1813:
** ἐξᾰλείφω**,
_perfect_ _passive_ ἐξαλήλιμμαι (see. below): _subjunctive_ _aorist 2_ _passive_ ἐξαλῐφῇ variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258b}}:— **plaster** or **wash over,** τοῦ σώματος τὸ ἥμισυ ἐξηλείφοντο γύψῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.69}}; ᾗ ἔτυχε.. οὐκ ἐξαληλιμμένον τὸ τεῖχος where it was not **whitewashed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20; τοὺς βωμοὺς ἐξαλείψαντι “IG” 11.161 A 103 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, **anoint,** μύρῳ βρενθείῳ ἐξαλείψαο {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.20.}}
**II** **wipe out, obliterate,** ἐξαλειφθεῖσ᾽ ὡς ἄγαλμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 262}}: _metaphorically_, **wipe out of one's mind,** πάντα τὰ πρόσθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187b; τὸ γιγνώσκειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.34}}; [ὑπόνοιαν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 310 (probably)}}; **cancel,** ἐ. ψηφίσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.76; νόμους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.48; αἰτίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 40.3}}; ἐξαλειφόντων (i.e. τὸ ὀφείλημα) {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.10}}; especially at Athens, ἐ. τινὰ ἐκ τοῦ καταλόγου **strike** his **name off** the roll, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.51, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 36.2; so ἐ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 877, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.39}}; opposed to ἐγγράφω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1181, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.2, etc.; ὑμᾶς ἐκ παντὸς τοῦ Ἑλληνικοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.57:}} —_middle_, ἐξαλείψασθαι τὰς ἀπογραφάς **to get** one's inventory **cancelled,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 850d: _metaphorically_, ἐ. πάθος φρενός}} **blot** it **out from** one's mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 590.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **wipe out, destroy,** μὴ 'ξαλείψῃς σπέρμα Πελοπιδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 503, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1241}}:—_passive_, ἡ Σπάρτης εὐδαιμονίη οὐκ ἐξηλείφετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220; τιμὰς μὴ 'ξαλειφθῆναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 15; οὐδ᾽ ἄπαις δόμος.. ἐξαλειφθείη ποτ᾽ ἄν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 698.}}

1814:
** ἐξάλλομαι**,
_future_ **-ᾰλοῦμαι**{{LXX|LXX.Mic.2.12}}: _aorist_ **-ηλόμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1311}} (Lyric poetry), **-ηλάμην** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 53}}, _Doric dialect_ **-άλατο**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus (see. below)}}; _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _participle_ -άλμενος (see. below):—**leap out of** or **forth from,** ἐξάλλεται αὐλῆς, of a lion, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.142: elsewhere used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_aorist_ _participle_ ἐξάλμενος, _absolutely_, {{Refs|15.571: with _genitive_, προμάχων ἐξάλμενος, τῶν ἄλλων ἐ.}} **springing out from the midst** of..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.342, 23.399 (not in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”); ἐξάλατο ναός 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.100}}; ἐ. κατὰ τοῦ τείχους **leap** down **off..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.6}}: _absolutely_, **jump, hop off,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 130, NT.Act.3.8}}; ὦ δαῖμον, ἵν᾽ ἐξήλο; **to** what point **didst thou leap forth,** i.e. to what misery hast thou come ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1311 (Lyric poetry)}}; of fish, **leap out of** the water, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 602a29, compare 528a32.}}
**2** **start from its socket, be dislocated,** of a limb, ἐ. ἔξαλσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 46}}; of a broken bone, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.341b}}; of wheels, **start from the axle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.32.}}
**II** **leap up,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “An.” 7.3.33; μήκιστα ἐ. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.318}}; of horses, **rear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.27.}}
**II.2** ἐξάλλετο γαστήρ **swelled, became distended,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 88 (assuming variant).}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, ἐ. πρός τι **fly off** to, **have recourse** to, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.382e.}}

1815:
** ἐξανά-στᾰσις**, εως, ἡ,
**removal, expulsion,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.21.9, +others}}
**II** _intransitive_, **emigration,** τινῶν ἐκ τῆς οἰκείας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.3.6.}}
**II.2** **rising from bed to go to stool,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 11}}; later simply, **going to stool,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.9 (_plural_), 4th c.AD: Severus Medicus “de Clysteribus” pp.3,34 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.}}
**II.2.b** **rising from bed in the morning,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Pythagorae - Porphyrii Opuscula” 40; ἐ. ὕπνου 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.96.}}
**II.3** ἡ ἐ. ἡ ἐκ νεκρῶν **resurrection** from the dead, {{NT|NT.Phil.3.11.}}
**II.4** woman's **ornament**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 717.11 (2nd c.AD).}}

1816:
** ἐξανα-τέλλω**,
**cause to spring up from,** ποίην χθονός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1423: _metaphorically_, θόρυβον ἐκ κεφαλῆς 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 44.}}
**2** _intransitive_, **spring up from,** χθονός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 62.4; ἀφ᾽ αἵματος 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.58.}}

1817:
** ἐξαν-ίστημι**:
**I** causal in _present_, _imperfect_, _future_, and _aorist_ 1:
**I.1** **raise up,** τοὺς θανόντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 940}}; **make** one **rise from his seat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310a}}; **bid** one **rise** from suppliant posture, ἐγώ σ᾽ ἕδρας ἐκ τῆσδε.. ἐξαναστήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 263, compare 267}}; ἐ. τὴν ἐνέδραν **order** the men in ambush **to rise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.37.}}
**I.2** **make** a tribe **emigrate, remove** or **expel,** ἐ. τινὰς ἐκ τῶν νήσων, ἐξ ἠθέων, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, 5.14, etc.; ἄνδρας δόμων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 297}}; ἐ. πόλεως **bid** one **depart from..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 47; simply ἐ. τινάς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.98, etc. (see. below 11.2).}}
**I.2.b** **challenge** a juror, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHal.” 9.5 (3rd c.BC), “PGurob” 2.10 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**I.3** **depopulate, destroy,** πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, +others; Ἰλίου ποτ᾽ ἐξαναστήσας βάθρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1198; Ἑλλάδα [prev. author] “Tr.” 926.}}
**I.4** ἐ. θηρία **rouse** them **from their lair,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.20.}}
**I.5** τουτὶ ἐ., **erigere penem,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 169.}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _passive_, with _aorist_ 2, _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _active_:
**II.1** **stand up from one's seat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142; ἐκ τοῦ θρόνου [prev. author] 5.72, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 211a; θάκων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.7}}; ὁδῶν τινί, in courtesy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.31}}; **rise** to speak, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 367}}; **rise** from ambush, λόχου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 217: without λόχου, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.107}}; **rise** after dinner, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328a, etc.; πρὸ μέθης 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.32; from bed, λέχους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 786; ἐξ εὐνῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.8}}; ἐξαναστῶμεν εἰς τὴν αὐλήν **let us rise and go** into.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a; εἰς περίπατον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.1.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, **arise and depart from, emigrate from,** Λακεδαίμονος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.49, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 380; ἐκ τῆς γῆς τῆσδε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.115}}: _absolutely_, **break up, depart,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.49, etc.}}
**II.3** **to be driven out** from one's home, **to be forced to emigrate,** ἐξ ἠθέων ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.15, compare 56, +others; πρὸς δάμαρτος ἐξανίσταται θρόνων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 767.}}
**II.4** of places, **to be depopulated,** ἐξαναστάσης πάσης Πελοποννήσου ὑπὸ Δωριέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.171; Τροίης ἐξανεστάθη βάθρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1652, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 16.25.}}
**II.5** **rise to go to stool,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.δ, etc.}}
**II.6** **rise from the plain,** of a mountain, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.56.4.}}
**II.6.b** so of ulcers, **rise,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.13}}; of an excrescence, κέρχνος ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 279.}}

1821:
** ἐξαπο-στέλλω**,
_future_ **-στελῶ**{{LXX|LXX.4Ki.8.12}}: _perfect_ ἐξαπέσταλκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Attalus Grammaticus (see. below)}}:—**dispatch,** πρεσβευτάς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.11.1; στρατηγόν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.102; θεωρούς “SIG” 629.8 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; βιβλίον τινί {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Attalus Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.3.3}}:—_passive_, **to be dispatched,** Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.77, “OGI” 90.20 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὑπό τινων {{Refs|“Vita Philonidis Epicurei” p.7C.}}; ἐξαπεσταλμένοι μάχεσθαι Aristeas 13; also ἡμῶν ὁ λόγος -εστάλη {{NT|NT.Act.13.26.}}
**2** **send forth,** [δαίμων] -στέλλων ὕδατα καὶ ἀνέμους {{Refs|“Sammelb.” 4324.16.}}
**3** of prisoners, **send** before a tribunal, ἐ. τινὰ δέσμιον πρός τινας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 22.18 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: —_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTaur.” 1iii13 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **send away, dismiss,** e.g. a prisoner, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.84.3}}; ἐ. τινὰ κενόν **send away** empty-handed, {{NT|NT.Luke.1.53}}; **divorce** a wife, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.24.4}}; **expel,** ἐκ τοῦ παραδείσον{{LXX|LXX.Gen.3.23.}}
**III** **discharge** a projectile, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 81.4 (_passive_).}}
**IV** **destroy,** ὀχυρώματα ἐν πυρί {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.8.12.}}
**V** **emit, display,** φαντασίαν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.72.}}

1822:
** ἐξαρτ-ίζω**,
**complete, finish,** τὰς ἡμέρας {{NT|NT.Act.21.5}}; **finish** a building, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).538 (from Mytilene)}}; [βιβλία] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 296.7 (1st c.AD):}} —_passive_, πόδες (i.e. τραπέζης) ἕως τῶν κάτω τελέως -ισμένοι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.6.6.}}
**II** **equip and dispatch,** σκάφας εἰς.. {{Refs|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 33:}} —_passive_, πλοῖα, γένη,{{Refs|[prev. work]19,14}}; simply, **equip,** ναῦς -ισμένας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.77}}; **furnish, supply,** Wilcken {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Chr.” 176.10 (1st c.AD)}}:— _passive_, ἐξηρτισμένον ἅπασι **completely furnished,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PAmh. 2.93.8 (2nd c.AD)}}; πρὸς πᾶν ἔργον ἀγαθὸν ἐξηρτισμένος {{NT|NT.2Tim.3.17}}: with _accusative_, **provide oneself with,** τὰ ἄλλα ἐξήρτιστο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.33.}}

1825:
** ἐξεγ-είρω**,
**awaken,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 65, “Tr.” 978}}:—_passive_, **to be awaked,** ὑπαὶ κώνωπος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 892}}; **wake up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1530}}: _aorist 2_ _middle_ ἐξηγρόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 51}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐξέγροντο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.21}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐξέγρετο {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; _infinitive_ ἐξεγρέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 223c; ἐξεγρόμενος [prev. passage]: so also}}_perfect_ _active_ ἐξεγρήγορα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1413: _2nd pers._ _sing._}}_aorist 2_ _passive_ ἐξέγρης· ἐξηγέρθης, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**2** **raise from the dead,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.6.14}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 495.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **awake, arouse,** εὕδοντα φόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 41; ἄνθρακα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 315; τὸν ἵππον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.12; πόλεμον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.44; ὁ ἄνεμος τὸ πῦρ ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 866a18.}}

1826:
** ἔξειμι**,
(εἰμί
**B** **sum**), only used in _impersonal_ forms (see. ἔξεστι), except in αἰ ἐλεύθεροι μὴ ἐξεῖεν if [a woman] shall leave no free-born **issue,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium 7.9.}}

1827:
** ἐξελ-έγχω**,
strengthened for ἐλέγχω, **convict, confute, refute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 75, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 297, “Ant.” 399, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1062; τοῖς ἔργοις τοὺς λόγους ἐ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.47}}; ἐν τῷ δήμῳ ἐ. [τινά] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.16}}:—_passive_, ἐπ᾽ αἰσχραῖς αἰτίαις ἐξελήλεγκται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.44; ὑπὸ τῶν εἰκότων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.9; ἔκ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 960; ἐξελεγχόμενος περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 304d; ὑπ᾽ ἐμοῦ ἐξελεγχθήσονται ἔργῳ [prev. author] “Ap.” 17b.}}
**2** with _double_ _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, **refute** one in a point,{{Refs|[prev. work] 23a, “Ly.” 222d:}} — _passive_, τοσοῦτον.. ἡλίκον οὗτος νῦν ἐξελήλεγκται **has been convicted of**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.147}}; οὐ τοῦτό γ᾽ ἐξελέγχομαι I **am** not **to blame** in this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 36.}}
**3** with predicate added in _participle_, **convict** one **of** being.., ἐ. τινὰ ἀδύνατον ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522d; ἐ. τινὰ τεχνάζοντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.19; ἐ. τινὰ ὡς οὐ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482b}}:—_passive_, ἵν᾽ ἐξελέγχοισθε πονηρευόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 125a; κἀξελέγχεται.. κάκιστος ὤν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 944; ἐξελέγχεται συμβεβουλευκώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.5, etc.}}
**II** **put to the proof, bring to the test,** ὁ ἐξελέγχων.. ἀλάθειαν χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).53}}; in a court of justice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 433; τὴν ποίησιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1366}}; ἐ. τὴν τύχην, τὰς ἐλπίδας, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.14.4, 1.62.4; ἐ. τοὺς Θηβαίους εἰ διαμαχοῦνται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 19:}} — _passive_, πάντες ἦσαν ἐξεληλεγμένοι all had **had their sentiments well ascertained,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.23}}; ἃ δ᾽ ἡ φύσις ἀεὶ ἐβούλετο, ἐξηλέγχθη ἐς τὸ ἀληθές **was fully proved** to be true, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64; χρυσὸς μὲν οἶδεν ἐξελέγχεσθαι πυρί 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 691.}}
**II.2** in Medicine texts, **find out one's weak points,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.902}}:— _passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.323.}}
**III** **compute,** χαλκὸν μυρίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.46.}}
**IV** **establish a claim to,** ὀγδοήκοντα τάλαντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.20.}}

1831:
** ἐξέρχομαι**,
_future_ -ελεύσομαι (but in _Attic dialect_ ἔξειμι (A) supplies the _future_, also _imperfect_ ἐξῄειν): _aorist 2_ ἐξῆλθον, the only tense used in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}:— **go** or **come out of,** with _genitive_ loci, τείχεος, πυλάων, πόληος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.237, 413, 417; ἐκ δ᾽ ἦλθε κλισίης 10.140}}; ἐ. δωμάτων, χθονός, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 663, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 778, etc.; ἐ. ἐκ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.75, 9.12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 37, etc.; ἔξω τῆσδ᾽.. χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 476}}; of an actor, **come out** on the stage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 240, “Av.” 512}}: _absolutely_, **come forth,** ἐ. καὶ ἀμῦναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.576.}}
**b** rarely with _accusative_, ἐξῆλθον τὴν Περσίδα χώραν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.29; ἐ. τὸ ἄστυ [prev. author] 5.104, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285a5, LXX.Gen.44.4.}}
**c** _absolutely_, **march out, go forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, etc.; ἐπί τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36.}}
**d** of an accused person, **withdraw from the country** to avoid trial, opposed to φεύγω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.45.}}
**e** ἐ. ὑπηρέτης to be commissioned to carry out an order of the court, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 89.36(2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**f** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **go out on** an expedition, etc., ἐ. ἐξόδους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.17; στρατείαν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.168}}; so παγκόνιτ᾽ ἐ. ἄεθλ᾽ ἀγώνων **went through** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 506 (Lyric poetry); νίκης ἔχων ἐξῆλθε.. γέρας [prev. author] “El.” 687.}}
**g** with Preps., ἐ. ἐπὶ θήραν, ἐπὶ θεωρίαν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52b, etc.}}; ἐπὶ πλεῖστον ἐ. **pursue their advantages** to the utmost, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; εἰς τόδ᾽ ἐ. ἀνόσιον στόμα **allow oneself to use** these impious words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 981}}; also ἐ. εἴς τινας **come out of** one class into another, as εἰς τοὺς τελείους ἄνδρας, opposed to ἔφηβοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.12.}}
**h** of disease, **pass off,** ἢν ἐκ τοῦ ἄλλου σώματος ἡ νοῦσος ἐξεληλύθῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.13.}}
**i** of offspring, **issue from** the womb, τὰ μὲν τετελειωμένα, τὰ δὲ ἀτελῆ ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 896a18; ἐκ τῆς γαστρός 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 9.3.}}
**i.2** ἐ. εἰς ἔλεγχον **stand forth and come** to the trial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 640; ἐς χερῶν ἅμιλλαν ἐ. τινί [prev. author] “Hec.” 226}}: _absolutely_, **stand forth, be proved to be,** ἄλλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1084}}; **come forth** (from the war), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31.}}
**i.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **execute,** ἃ ἂν.. μὴ ἐξέλθωσιν (variant for{ἐπεξ-}) {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.70}}; τὸ πολὺ τοῦ ἔργου ἐξῆλθον (variant for{ἐπεξ-}) {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.108.}}
**i.4** _absolutely_, **exceed all bounds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 644b; so ἐ. τὰ νόμιμα 3rd c.BC: Nymphis Historicus 15.}}
**i.5** with _accusative_ of the instrument of motion, ἐ. οὐδὲ τὸν ἕτερον πόδα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.82.}}
**II** of Time, **come to an end, expire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.139, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 735, “PRev.Laws” 48.9 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; τοῦ ἐξελθόντος μηνός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 35; ἐπειδὰν.. ὁ ἐνιαυτὸς ἐξέλθῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298e; ἐλέγοντο αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐξεληλυθέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.2.}}
**II.2** of magistrates, etc., **go out of office,** ἡ ἐξελθοῦσα βουλή Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.77, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273a16.}}
**III** of prophecies, dreams, events, etc., **to be accomplished, come true,** ἐς τέλος ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 218: _absolutely_, τὴν ὄψιν συνεβάλετο ἐξεληλυθέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108, compare 82}}; ἐξῆλθε (i.e. ἡ μῆνις) **was satisfied,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.137; ἰσόψηφος δίκη ἐξῆλθ᾽ ἀληθῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 796}}; κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν ἐ. **come out** right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 88; ἀριθμὸς οὐκ ἐλάττων ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.5}}; of persons, μὴ.. Φοῐβος ἐξέλθη σαφής **turn out** a true prophet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1011.}}
**III.2** of words, **proceed,** παρά τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b}}; of goods, **to be exported,** {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus1.122e.}}

1833:
** ἐξετ-άζω**,
_future_ ἐξετάσω, rarely ἐξετῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.34, compare “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 251}}: _aorist_ ἐξήτασα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 438, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 211 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc., _Doric dialect_ ἐξήταξα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.28}}: _perfect_ ἐξήτακα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154d, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ετασθήσομαι**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.20:}}_aorist_ -ητάσθην (see. below): _perfect_ -ήτασμαι (see. entry near the end):—**examine well** or **closely, scrutinize, review,** ἐ. φίλους, ὅντιν᾽ ἔχουσι νόον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1016, cf. Ar.[same place], etc.; τὴν ὑπάρχουσαν ξυμμαχίαν ἐ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7; βίον αὐτοῦ πάντα ἐξετάσω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.21; ἐκ τοῦ εἰκότος ἐξετασθῆναι δεῖ τὸ πρᾶγμα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.37}}; ἐ., opposed to ὑπέχειν λόγον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a5}}; τὸ δι᾽ ἀκριβείας -αζόμενον exactly **weighed** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 184c}}; ἐ. τι [διὰ] τῶν εἰδότων **make inquiries into** a thing **from..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.8.1: followed by Relat., ἐ. ὅστις ἦν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.82; ἐ. τί καὶ πῶς λέγουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 261a; ἐ. τινά, τίνος ἐστὶ γένους 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.17.}}
**2** of troops, **inspect, review,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.33, 35, etc.; στρατιώτας σὺν τοῖς ὅπλοις “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 10.1}}:—_passive_, στρατὸς δὲ θάσσει κἀξετάζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 391, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.97.}}
**3** ἐ. τὴν βουλήν, τὸ βουλευτικόν, = Latin _legere senatum_, **revise the roll of** the Senate, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.42, 54.13.}}
**4** **examine, approve,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 40.19 (_passive_, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**5** **pass in review, enumerate,** ἁμαρτήματα ἀκριβῶς ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.63, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.52,58.}}
**II** **examine** or **question** a person **closely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 586, “OC” 211; τινὰ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258d; τινά τι [prev. author] “Grg.” 515b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.35; δικαίως αὐτὸν ἐξετάσω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.154, compare 18.20; τὸν δεσπότην ὁ δοῦλος ἐξετάζει [prev. author] 45.76}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 65.}}
**III** **estimate,** τι πρός τι one thing with reference to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.7; πρὸς ἐκείνους ἐ. καὶ παραβάλλειν ἐμέ [prev. author] 18.314; ἰσοστάσιος ἦν ἡ πορφύρα πρὸς ἄργυρον ἐξεταζομένη 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 114, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.119a; ἐ. τινὰ παρ᾽ ἄλληλα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.265, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.11}}; **compare,** πρὸς Ἀριστογείτονα ἐμαυτόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.43.}}
**IV** **prove by scrutiny** or **test,** of gold, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Chilo Lyricus 1 (_passive_)}}; ἐ. τοὺς κακούς τε κἀγαθούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.14; τοὺς χρησίμους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.38}}: with _participle_, ἐξητακὼς στερεοὺς ὑπάρχοντας τοὺς τόπους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.79.1:}} —more frequently in _passive_, ἐὰν μὴ παρὼν ἐ. unless he is **proved** to have been present, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 764a; καὶ λέγων καὶ γράφων ἐξηταζόμην τὰ δέοντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.173; ἐξήτασαι πεποιηκώς [prev. work]197; ἐξετάζεσθαι φίλος (i.e. ὤν) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1011; ἐχθρὸς ἐξεταζόμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.65; κατήγορος [prev. author] 22.66; μέτριοι ἐν τοῖς ἀνηκέστοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.74b}}; of things, τὰ φοβερὰ ἐξετασθήσεται μέχρι λόγου τοιαῦτα ὄντα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.63.}}
**V** _passive_, **to be numbered, counted,** with _genitive_, ὧν εἷς ἐγὼ βουληθεὶς ἐξετάζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.2; τῶν ἐχθρῶν εἷς ἐξετάζεσθαι}}**to be found in the number of..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.291}}; μετὰ τῶν ἄλλων ἐξητάζετο he **appeared among..,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.217; ἔν τισι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.59}}; **to be placed on a roll,** ἐν τοῖς ἱππικοῖς among the {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Equites” at Rome, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 14}}; of the census, ἐξητάσθησαν αἱ πᾶσαι πεντεκαίδεκα [μυριάδες] {{Refs|[prev. author]}}**caes.**{{Refs|55.}}
**V.2** _passive_, **present oneself, appear,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.161; πρὸς τὸν ἄρχοντα.. οὐδέπω.. ἐξήτασται [prev. author] 37.46, compare 18.277.}}

1834:
** ἐξηγ-έομαι**,
**to be leader of,** with _genitive_ _person_, τῶν δ᾽ ἐξηγείσθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.806 (for 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.116, see 11.3).}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_, **lead, govern,** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, τὰς πόλεις 1.76; τὴν Πελοπόννησον [prev. work] 71.}}
**2.b** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.151, 9.11.}}
**3** with _dative_ _person_ et _accusative_ _of things_, **show** one **the way to,** τοῖσι ἐχθροῖσι τῆς πατρίδος ἅλωσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.135; ἃ δ᾽ ἐξηγεῖσθε τοῖς ξυμμάχοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.55}}: with _dative_ _person_ only, **go before, lead,** ἡμῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1589, etc.}}: with _accusative_ loci only, **lead the way to,** χῶρον {{Refs|[prev. work] 1520.}}
**4** with _genitive_ _of things_, ἐ. τῆς πράξεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.29; with _dative_ _person_ added, πᾶσι κάλλους τε καὶ τελειότητος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.132d.}}
**5** ἐ. εἰς τὴν Ἑλλάδα **lead an army** into Greece, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.34.}}
**II** **dictate** a form of words, ἐ. τὸν νόμον τῷ κήρυκι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.70}}; ἐξηγοῦ θεούς **dictate, name** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 745.}}
**II.2** generally, **prescribe, order,** ποιήσουσι τοῦτο τὸ ἂν κεῖνος ἐξηγέηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23; ὅ τι χρὴ ποιέειν ἐξηγέο σύ [prev. author] 4.9, compare 7.234; ᾗ ὁ νόμος ἐξηγεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604b}}: of a diviner, with _infinitive_, **order** one to do, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 595; τἄλλα δ᾽ ἐξηγοῦ φίλοις [prev. author] “Ch.” 552}}; especially frequently of religious forms and ceremonies, οἷς τῶν ἄλλων θεῶν οἱ Μάγοι ἐξηγοῦντο, ={τοῖς ἄλλοις θεοῖς οὕς}.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.11, compare 4.5.51, 7.3.1; τί φ; δίδασκ᾽ ἄπειρον ἐξηγουμένη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 118, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1284, etc.; οὗτος ὁ θεὸς περὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα.. ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427c, compare 469a.}}
**II.3** **expound, interpret,** ἐ. τὸ οὔνομα καὶ τὴν θυσίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.49; τὸν ποιητήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 407a; ἃ Ὅμηρος λέγει [prev. author] “Ion” 531a; ὁ τὸν Ἡράκλειτον.. ἐξηγούμενος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 113.3; τὰ νόμιμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.69: _absolutely_, ἄγραφοι νόμοι καθ᾽ οὓς Εὐμολπίδαι ἐξηγοῦνται}} according to which they **expound** things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.116 (to be read κηρύκων ὤν);compare ἐξηγητής 11.}}
**III** **tell at length, relate in full,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.3, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 216, 702, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26}}; **set forth, explain,** τὴν ἔλασιν the line of march, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.4, 7.6; ἃ μετὰ χεῖρας ἔχοι καὶ -ήσασθαι οἷός τε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138; τὰ τοῦ νομοθέτου βουλήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802c, compare “R.” 474c}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **explain** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 320: followed by _relative_, ἐ. ὁτέῳ τρόπῳ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72. etc.; ἐ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.1.}}

1836:
** ἑξῆς**,
_Epic dialect_ **ἑξείης**, _adverb_, _Doric dialect_ **ἑξαν** (accent unknown), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).155.108 (Rhodes, 2nd c.BC), “SIG” 1023.80 (Cos, 3rd-2nd c.BC), +others}}:— **one after another, in order, in a row,** ἑξῆς εὐνάζοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.449; ἑξῆς δ᾽ ἑζόμενοι [prev. work] 580 (elsewhere 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses the form ἑξείης, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.241, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.408); πάντας ἑ... κτείνοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 657.2}}; τὰ ἑ. variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 310b12.}}
**b** in Mathematics texts, ἑ. ἀνάλογον in **continued** proportion, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 8.1, +others}}; οἳ ἑ. ἀριθμοί **successive** numbers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra}}**Spir.Praef.;** γραμμαὶ ἑ. κείμεναι placed **in order,** {{Refs|[prev. work]11}}; τούτου ἑ. γινομένου if this be done **continually,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.Cyl.” 1.11.}}
**2** ἑ. διεξελθεῖν, λέγειν, **in a regular, consequential manner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 257b, 286c; τοῦ ἑ. ἕνεκα περαίνεσθαι τὸν λόγον [prev. author] “Grg.” 454c}}; ὁ ἑ. λόγος the **following** argument, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 20b; ἐν ἅπασι τούτοις ἑ. 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 9.14, compare 4.4.}}
**3** Grammars, τὸ ἑ. **grammatical sequence,** opposed to ὑπερβατόν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 41.3, +others}}; καὶ τὰ ἑ., Latin _et cetera,_ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PTeb. 319.34 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**4** of Time, **thereafter, next,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 475, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 638; τὸν ἑ. χρόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271b; ἡ ἑ. ἡμέρα NT.Luke.9.37}}; ἐν τῷ (variant{τῇ}) ἑ. **next day,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.11}}; εἰς τὸ ἑ. for the **future,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 474.28 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**4.b** of Place, **next,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 249, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392a26.}}
**II** c._genitive_, **next to,** τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 765; τὰ τούτων ἑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 390a; τούτων ἑ.}}**next after..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.102}}; of logical connexion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 42c}}: with _dative_, **next to,** Λάχητι.. τὴν ἑ. θύραν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 16}}; τούτοις ἑ. **next in order to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399d, +others}} τὸ ἑ. τῇ γεωμετριᾳ **what comes next to..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 528a}}; τὸ ἑ. ἔργον τοῖς Μαραθῶνι **next after,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mx.” 241a}}; ἑ. Ἀριστογείτονι **beside** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 633}}; παρὰ τὸ ἑ. τῷ νοερῷ ζῴῳ **that which befits..,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.5}}; ἐπεχορήγησα αὐτῇ τὰ ἑ. made **suitable** provision for her, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 282.7 (1st c.AD).}}

1837:
** ἐξηχ-έω**,
**sound forth,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX “Joel” 3(4).14, 2nd c.BC(?): Nechepso Astrologus cited in 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 241.17: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τὸ κύκνειον ἐξηχεῖν}} **sound forth** the swan's song, i.e. **give vent to** dying prayers, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.4.7, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.24}}:—_passive_, {{NT+5th c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.107, NT.1Thes.1.8, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**II** **utter senseless sounds,** of idiots, {{Refs|Polemo Physiognomonicus 51.}}

1838:
** ἕξις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἔχω):
**I** (ἔχω _transitive_) **having, being in possession of, possession,** ἐπιστήμης ἕ., opposed to κτῆσις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197b; νοῦ [prev. author] “Cra.” 414b; ἡ τῶν ὅπλων [prev. author] “Lg.” 625c, compare “R.” 433e, “Sph.” 247a, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1022b4}}; opposed to στέρησις,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1055b13, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.49.}}
**I.2** in surgery, **posture,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 3; ἕ. ἢ θέσις [prev. work] 15.}}
**II** (ἔχω _intransitive_) **a being in a certain state, a permanent condition** as produced by practice (πρᾶξις), different from σχέσις (which is alterable) (see. below):
**II.1** **state** or **habit** of body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aph.” 2.34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153b}}; ἕ. ὑγιεινή (so also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.4), opposed to διάθεσις ἀθλητική, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 34; σχέσις καὶ ἕ. καὶ ἡλικίη [prev. author] “Mochl.” 41; ἡ φύσις καὶ ἡ ἕ. [prev. author] “Acut.” 43: _plural_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 69}}: generally, **condition,** ἐν ἕξει τοῦ δρᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἕ. λεπτὴ κατὰ τοῦτο τὸ μέρος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 12; τῷ θερμὴν ἕ. ἔχοντι 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus p.26W.}}; **outward appearance,** ἡ ἕ. τοῦ σώματος κρείσσων {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Dan.1.15, cf. LXX.1Ki.16.7, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Lam.4.7}}; **habit** of a vine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.14.5}}; of material objects, ὑπὸ μιᾶς ἕξεως συνέχεσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.102, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.511, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.124, +others}}
**II.1.b** _medical_, **the system,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.45e, Mnesith. [prev. work] 54b, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 3.59.}}
**II.2** **state** or **habit of mind,** ἕ. κακίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 184; τὰς φύσεις τε καὶ ἕξεις τῶν ψυχῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 650b, etc.}}; ἡ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ ἕ., opposed to ἡ τῶν σωμάτων ἕ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.”}}[same place]; πονηρᾶς ψυχῆς ἕξει{{Refs|[prev. work] 167b; λαμβάνειν ἕξιν τιμιωτέραν [prev. author] “R.” 591b.}}
**II.2.b** especially **acquired habit,** opposed to ἐνέργεια, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098b33, +others}}
**II.3** **trained habit, skill,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 955b1, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.47.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.29}}; τέχνη defined as ἕ. ἢ διάθεσις ἀπὸ παρατηρήσεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.69S.; ἄκρα ἕ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 11: with _genitive_, τὴν τῶν Ἰουδαϊκῶν γραμμάτων ἕξιν Aristeas 121; ἕ. πολιτικῶν λόγων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.35 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} (Almost confined to Prose, but{{Refs 5th c.AD+|compare 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 391.)}}

1839:
** ἐξίστημι**,
causal in _present_, _imperfect_, _future_, _aorist_ 1:—**displace**: hence, **change, alter utterly,** τὰν φύσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119a23, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.7; τὴν πολιτείαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 10; ἐ. τῆς ποιότητος τὸν οἶνον [prev. author] 2.702a.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἐξιστάναι τινὰ φρενῶν **drive** one **out of** his senses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 850; νοῦ οἶνος ἐξέστησέ με 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 265; τοῦ φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.12; ταῦτα κινεῖ, ταῦτα ἐξίστησιν ἀνθρώπους αὑτῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.72}}; simply ἐ. τινά **drive** one **out of his senses, confound, amaze,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 429; ἐξιστάντα καὶ φοβοῦντα τοὺς ἀνθρώπους 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 8p.35H.; ἐξίστησι}}**diverts the attention,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408b23}}; **excite,**{{NT|[prev. work] 36, NT.Luke.24.22}}; τὸν λογισμόν, τὴν διάνοιαν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 21, “Crass.” 23; also ἐ. τινὰ τῶν λογισμῶν [prev. author] “Fab.” 5; εἰς ἀπάθειαν ἐ. τὴν ψυχήν [prev. author] “Publ.” 6.}}
**3** **get rid of, dispose of the claims** of a person, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 5246.14 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**4** ἐξεστᾰκότα (ἐξεστηκότα codex): εἰς δίκην κεκληκότα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**B** _intransitive_ in _passive_ and _middle_, with _aorist_ 2, _perfect_, and _pluperfect_ _active_:
**B.1** of Place, **arise out of, become separated,** ἐξ.. ἵστατο Νεῖκος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 36, compare 35.10}}; **stand aside from,** ἐκστάντες τῆς ὁδοῦ **out of** the way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.76; ἐκ τοῦ μέσου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.14}}; θάκων καὶ ὁδῶν ἐ. [τινί] **stand out of** the way **for**him, **make way for** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.31; ἐκστῆναί τινι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1053, “Aj.” 672, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 354, etc.}}: _absolutely_, in same sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1229 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 617, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἐξ ἕδρας σοι πλόκαμος ἐξέστηχ᾽}}**is displaced, disordered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 928; οὐδὲ μένει νοῦς.. ἀλλ᾽ ἐξίσταται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 564.}}
**B.2** with _accusative_, **shrink from, shun,** νιν οὐκ ἂν ἐξέστην ὄκνῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 82; οὐδέν᾽ ἐξίσταμαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.319; οὐδένα πώποτε κίνδυνον ἐξέστησαν [prev. author] 20.10.}}
**B.3** **go out of joint,** ἐ. ἰσχίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 6.59, compare “Fract.” 14, 6.}}
**B.II** with _genitive_ _of things_, **retire from, give up possession of,** τῆς ἀρχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63, 4.28}}; ἐκστῆναι τῆς οὐσίας, ἁπάντων τῶν ὄντων, become bankrupt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.50; τῶν ὑπαρχόντων “BGU” 473.11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**B.II.2** **cease from, abandon,** τῆς φιλίας, τῶν μαθημάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.54}}; τῶν σπουδασμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249c, etc.; οἱ τῶν πολιτικῶν ἐξεστηκότες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.171; τῆς ὑποθέσεως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.46}}; τῶν πεπραγμένων, i.e. **disown** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.72; ἐ. τινὸς εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 907d}}; also ἐ. ἄθλου τινί, στρατηγίας τινί, **abandon** it in his favour, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 73J., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 7; τῆς Σικελίας τινι [prev. author] “Pomp.” 10.}}
**B.II.3** ἐκστῆναι πατρός **lose** one's father, **give** him **up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 477}}; καρδίας ἐξίσταμαι τὸ δρᾶν I **depart from** my heart's purpose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1105}}; especially φρενῶν ἐκστῆναι **lose** one's senses. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1021, etc.; διὰ τὸ γῆρας τοῦ φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.18; ἐμαυτοῦ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 276; ψυχὴ ἐξεστηκυῖα τῶν λογισμῶν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.15.8}}: _absolutely_, **to be out of one's wits, be distraught,** ἐ. μελαγχολικῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.18, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 64, etc.; ἐξέστην ἰδών 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 27; ἐ. ὑπὸ γήρως “Comica Adespota” 860}}; ταῖς διανοίαις Vett. Val.70.25; ἐξίστασθαι καὶ μαίνεσθαι πρὸς τὴν ὀσμήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 577a12}}; of anger, εὐθέως ἐξστησόμενος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.78 W.}}; **to be astonished, amazed,** {{NT|NT.Matt.12.23, NT.Mark.2.12, etc.}}; **lose consciousness,** of Sisera, {{LXX|LXX.Josh.4.21.}}
**B.II.4** ἐξίστασθαι τῆς αὑτοῦ ἰδέας **depart from, degenerate from** one's own nature, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380d; ἐκ τῆς αὑτοῦ φύσεως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b19; [δημοκρατία] ἐξεστηκυῖα τῆς βελτίστης τάξεως [prev. author] “Pol.” 1309b32}}; αἱ δημοκρατίαι ἐ. εἰς τὰς ἐναντίας πολιτείας **degenerate** into..,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1306b18, compare “Rh.” 1390b28: _absolutely_, ἐ. μὴ μεταφυτευόμενον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.7.6, etc., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.649e; χυμὸς ἐξιστάμενος}}**changing its properties, turning,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 24}}; οἶνος ἐξεστηκώς or ἐξιστάμενος **changed, sour** wine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.32, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.7.5}}; πρόσωπα ἐξεστηκότα **disfigured** faces, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.34.}}
**B.II.5** _absolutely_, **change one's position, one's opinion,** ἐγὼ μὲν ὁ αὐτός εἰμι καὶ οὐκ ἐξίσταμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61: opposed to ἐμμένειν τῇ δόξῃ, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1151b4.}}
**B.II.6** of language, **to be removed from common usage,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1404b13.}}
**B.III** **stand out, project,** ἐξεστηκός **convex,** opposed to κοῖλον, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 493b4.}}

1840:
** ἐξισχύω** [ῡ],
**have strength enough, be able,** ὥστε ποιεῖν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.3}}: with _infinitive_ only, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Sir.7.6, NT.Eph.3.18, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.23.2}}; ἐξίσχυσεν τὰ βιβλείδια ἀθετηθῆναι **procured** the rejection of the petition, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1120.7 (3rd c.AD)}}: _absolutely_, **prevail,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.1.15, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.160c; ἐξισχῦσαι καὶ κρατῆσαι τῶν πολλῶν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.801e.}}
**II** with _genitive_, τὸ δαιμόνιον παίδων ἐξισχῦον fate **prevailing over** the children, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 6.13, compare 7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 1.71D.}}
**III** _middle_, of flames, **gather force,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 71.}}

1843:
** ἐξομολογ-έομαι**,
**confess,** τὰς ἁμαρτίας {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Dan.9.20, NT.Matt.3.6, +others, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.4.6}}; **admit, acknowledge,** μυθογραφίαν {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.35; ἧτταν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 17; πίστεις “PGnom.” 18; ὅτι.. NT.Phil.2.11, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 75; διότι.. LXX.2Mac.7.37}}; especially in legal formulae, ἐ. εἰληφέναι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PAvrom.” 1.7 (1st c.BC)}}; **acknowledge,** υἱόν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1473.9 (3rd c.AD)}}: _absolutely_, **acknowledge a liability,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.30.18 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **make grateful acknowledgements, give thanks, sing praises,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.2Ki.22.50, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.59, +others, NT.Matt.11.25: with _accusative_, τοῦτο τῷ Κυρίῳ LXX.Gen.29.35.}}
**II** later in _active_, **agree, consent,** {{NT|NT.Luke.22.6:—}}_passive_, ἐξωμολογημέναι ἀποδείξεις **agreed, admitted** proofs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 685.95 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC).}}

1844:
** ἐξορκ-ίζω**,
={ἐξορκόω}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.26 (codices, -οῦντες 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus), “PRev.Laws” 56.12 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.61.10, “GDI” 5075.25 (Crete, 1st c.BC)}}, etc.: with _double_ _accusative_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 524.29 (Crete, 3rd c.BC):}} —_passive_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]46.6 (Halic., 5th c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.26.4, “IG” 22.1346.}}
**2** **conjure,** ἐ. σε κύριον τὸν θεόν {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.24.3, compare “PMag.Lond.” 121.269; ἐ. [τινὰ] τοῖς μεγάλοις ὀνόμασιν [prev. work] 892; ἐ. σε κατὰ τοῦ θεοῦ NT.Matt.26.63; also τινὰ κατά τινος “PMag.Par.” 1.356.}}
**II** **exorcise** an evil spirit, {{Refs|“Tab.Defix.” in “Rh.Mus.” 55.248.}}

1846:
** ἐξορύσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐξορύττω**, **dig out** the earth from a trench, τὸν ἀεὶ ἐξορυσσόμενον χοῦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.23; τόποι ἐξορυσσόμενοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 833b4:}} — _middle_, ἐξορύξασθαι χάρακας **make oneself** a vallum, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.55.}}
**II** **dig out** of the ground, **dig up,** τοὺς νεκρούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64, compare “BGU” 1024iv4 (4th-5th c.AD)}}; ἄγλιθας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 763}}; [μορίαν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.26}}:—_passive_, τοῦ χοὸς τοῦ -ομένου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHal.” 1.109 (3rd c.BC)}}; φυτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.4.}}
**II.2** **gouge out,** ἐ. αὐτῶν τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.116, cf. LXX.Judg.16.21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 14.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, τὸν ἐξορύσσοντα λόγον τὰ κεκρυμμένα τῶν πραγμάτων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.72.}}

1849:
** ἐξουσ-ία**, ἡ,
(ἔξεστι) **power, authority** to do a thing, with _infinitive_, χαίρειν καὶ νοσεῖν ἐ. πάρεστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 88.11 codices; αὐτῷ ἐ. ἦν σαφῶς εἰδέναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.6, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.12}}; ἐξουσίαν ὁ νόμος δέδωκε **permission** to do.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182e; ἐ. ποιεῖν [prev. author] “Cri.” 51d, etc.; ἐ. λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.24, etc.; λαβὼν ἐ. ὥστε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.45}}; ἐπὶ τῇ τῆς εἰρήνης ἐ. with the **freedom permitted** by peace, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.44: with _genitive_ objecti, ἐ. ἔχειν θανάτου}} **power** of life and death, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.86}}; πρᾶγμα οὗ τὴν ἐ. ἔχουσιν ἄλλοι **control** over.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 57}}; ἐ. τινός **power over, licence in** a thing, τοῦ λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 461e}}; ἐν μεγάλῃ ἐ. τοῦ ἀδικεῖν{{Refs|[prev. work] 526a, compare “R.” 554c}}; κατὰ τὴν οὐκ ἐ. τῆς ἀγωνίσεως from want of **qualification for..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.50}}: _absolutely_, **power, authority,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 784.}}
**2** **abuse of authority, licence, arrogance,** ὕβρις καὶ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.38, compare 3.45, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.200; ἡ ἄγαν ἐ. [prev. work]272; ἄμετρος ἐ. “OGI” 669.51 (1st c.AD).}}
**3** Lit. Crit., ἐ. ποιητική poetic **licence,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.17, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.10b.}}
**II** **office, magistracy,** ἀρχαὶ καὶ ἐ. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.135b; οἱ ἐν ταῖς ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1095b21; οἱ ἐν ἐ. ὄντες [prev. author] “Rh.” 1384a1; οἱ ἐπ᾽ ἐξουσιῶν LXX.Dan.3.2}}; ἡ ὑπατικὴ ἐ. the **consulate,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.113, etc.; also ἡ ὕπατος ἐ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.1}}; ἡ ταμιευτικὴ ἐ. the **quaestorship,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.77}}; δημαρχικὴ ἐ., see at {δημαρχικό; ἡ τοῦ θαλάμου ἐ}., in the Roman empire, **lordship** of the bedchamber, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.12.3.}}
**II.2** concrete, **body of magistrates,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.32}}; αἱ ἐ. (as we say) the **authorities,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.12.11, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 17.}}
**II.2.b** ἡ ἐ. as an honorary title, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1103 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**III** **abundance of means, resources,** ἐξουσίας ἐπίδειξις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31; πλοῦτος καὶ ἐ. [prev. author] 1.123, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.138; ἐνδεεστέρως ἢ πρὸς τὴν ἐ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.39; τῶν ἀναγκαίων ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 828d}}; **excessive wealth,** opposed to οὐσία, {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 25a.5D.}}
**IV** **pomp,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 34.}}

1850:
** ἐξουσ-ιάζω**,
_future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus (see. below)}}:—**exercise authority,** {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.8.4}}: with _infinitive_, **have power,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.6S., 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.44.}}
**2** **exercise authority over,** τοῦ μνήματος {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4584 (from Palestine)}}; τινῶν {{NT+6th c.AD+|NT.Luke.22.25, cf. NT.1Cor.7.4; τῶν ἑαυτῆς ἕδνων “PMasp.” 15.170 (6th c.AD)}}:—_middle_, ἐπὶ τὸν λαόν {{LXX|LXX.Neh.5.15}}; ἔν τινι{{LXX|LXX.Eccl.8.9}}:— _passive_, **to be held under authority,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.6.12.}}
**3** **enjoy licence,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1216a2.}}

1851:
** ἐξοχ-ή**, ἡ,
(ἐξέχω) **prominence,** ἐ. κεράτων **elevated nature,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 663a8; πέτρας LXX.Job.39.28}}; ζῴων ἐξοχαί **embossed figures** on shields, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.30; εἰσοχαὶ καὶ ἐ. 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.120, compare 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 409.13}}; **wart,** [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.104; ἐ. ἀκανθώδεις [prev. author] 3.16; also, ={ἐξοχάδες}, [prev. work]80}}] **extremities** of animals, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.10.3.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **pre-eminence,** ἐ. **in nullo est,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 4.15.7; ἀπεργάσασθαι τὴν ἐ. 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 10.3}}; δι᾽ ἐξοχὴν μορφῆς Hierocl.p.55 {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus; κατ᾽ ἐξοχήν}}**par excellence,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.10, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.65, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 26.15, “OGI” 764.52 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; οἱ κατ᾽ ἐξοχὴν τῆς πόλεως **leading** men, {{NT|NT.Act.25.23.}}

1854:
** ἔξω**,
_adverb_ of ἐξ, as εἴσω of εἰς:
**I** of Place,
**I.1** with Verbs of motion, **out** or **out of,** ἔ. ἰών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.526; χωρεῖν ἔ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10; πορεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247b; βλέπειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.323}}; ἔ. τοὺς χριστιανούς (i.e. φέρε) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 38, etc.}}
**I.1.b** as _preposition_, with _genitive_, ἔ. χροὸς ἕλκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.457; ἔ. βήτην μεγάροιο κιόντε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.378}}; ἔ. or γῆς ἔ. βαλεῖν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1019, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 622, etc.: pleonastic with ἐκ, κραδίη δέ τοι ἔ. στηθέων ἐκθρῴσκει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.94; ἐκ τῆς ταφῆς ἐκφέρειν ἔ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.16, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 650: ἐκπλώσαντες ἔ. τὸν Ἑλλήσποντον}} sailing **outside** the H., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.103; ἔ. τὸν Ἑλλ. πλέων 7.58.}}
**I.2** without any sense of motion, **outside,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.95, etc.}}; τὸ ἔ. the **outside,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.69}}; τὸ ἔ. τῶν ὀμμάτων their **prominency,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143e}}; τὰ ἔ. **things outside** the walls or house, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.30}}; **external things,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 198c}}; τὰ ἔ. πράγματα **foreign** affairs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.68}}; οἱ ἔ. those **outside,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.14}}; of exiles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.66, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 444}} (but in **NT,** the **heathen,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Cor.5.12); ἡ ἔ. στηλέων θάλασσα ἡ Ἀτλαντὶς καλεομένη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 108e}}; ἡ ἔ. θάλασσα, opposed to ἡ εἴσω, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 40(5).9}}; ἔ. τὴν χεῖρα ἔχειν keep one's arm **outside** one's cloak, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.25.}}
**I.2.b** as _preposition_, with _genitive_, οἱ ἔ. γένους, opposed to τὰ ἐγγενῆ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 660; ἔ. τῶν κακῶν οἰκεῖν [prev. author] “OT” 1390}}; ἔ. τοξεύματος **out of range of** arrows, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.30}}; ἔ. βελῶν, τῶν β., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.69, “An.” 5.2.26}}; ἔ. τοῦ πολέμου **unconcerned with** the war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; τοῦ πάσχειν κακῶς ἔ. γενήσεσθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.34}}; τῶν ἔ. τοῦ πράγματος ὄντων persons **unconcerned in** the matter, {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.45, compare [prev. work]15}}; πράξεις ἔ. τῆς ὑποθέσεως λεγομένας **away from** the subject, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.74; ἔ. τοῦ πράγματος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a22}}; ἔ. τοῦ δικαστηρίου [ἔπαινοι] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 59}}; ἔ. λόγου τίθεσθαι, θέσθαι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.671a, “Tim.” 36}}; ἔ. πάτου ὀνόματα **out-of**-the-way words, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 44}}; ἔ. πίστεως **beyond** belief, {{Refs|[prev. author] “DMar.” 4.1}}; ἔ. φρενῶν **out of** one's senses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus O 7.47; ἔ. ἐλαύνειν τοῦ φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 853; ἔ. σαυτοῦ γίγνῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 535b; ἔ. γνώμης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 926}}; οὐδὲν ἔ. τοῦ φυτεύσαντος δρᾷς **unlike** thy sire, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 904}}; ἔ. τῆς ἀνθρωπείας.. νομίσεως **alien to** human belief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105: proverbial, αἴρειν ἔ. πόδα πηλοῦ}} keep **clear** of difficulties, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus; so ἔ. κομίζων πηλοῦ πόδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 697; πημάτων ἔ. πόδα ἔχειν [prev. author] “Pr.” 265; ἔ. πραγμάτων ἔχειν πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 109.}}
**II** of Time, **beyond, over,** ἔ. μέσου ἡμέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.4.1; ἔ. τῆς ἡλικίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.34; ἔ. πέντ᾽ ἐτῶν [prev. author] 38.18.}}
**III** **without, except,** with _genitive_, ἔ. σεῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.29, compare 4.46; ἔ. ἤ.. [prev. author] 2.3, 7.228; ἔ. τοῦ πλεόνων ἄρξαι}}**besides..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.97, compare 26}}; ἔ. τοῦ ἐφθακέναι ἀδικοῦντες **except** the being first to do wrong, Epist. Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.39, compare “PSI” 6.577.17, “PCair.Zen.” 225.4.}}
**IV** τὰ κατὰ τὸν Φίλιππον ἔ. τελέως ἐστί, Philip is 'played out', {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.28.4.}} — Cf. ἐξωτέρω, -τάτω.

1855:
** ἔξωθεν**,
rarely **ἔξωθε** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 18}}, _adverb_, (ἔξω) **from without** or **abroad,** ἔ. εἴσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 560, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 293d, etc.; ἔ. εἰστρέχειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 37.}}
**II** ={ἔξω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 33c, etc.}}; οἱ ἔ. those **outside,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.5, etc.}} (but **heathen** in {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.1Tim.3.7); οἱ ἔ. περιεστηκότες 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.5}}; τὰ ἔ. **matters outside the house,** opposed to τἄνδον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 201, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 74, etc.}}; αἱ ἔ. πόλεις **foreign** states, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 307e; οἱ ἔ. λόγοι}}**foreign to the subject,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.9; ἀκαταξέστους ἐκ τοῦ ἔ. “IG” 12.372.61.}}
**II.b** with _genitive_, ἐντὸς ἢ ἔ. δόμω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1312; ἔ. ὅπλων συγκαθήμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.24}}; **free from,** ξυμφορᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1449; δειμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 723.}}
**II.c** with _genitive_, **besides, apart from,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.409, 16.502.}}
**III** Grammars, ἔ. προσλαμβάνειν **supply** or **understand** a word, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 107.3; προσνεῖμαι [prev. work] 92.1; ὑπακούεσθαι [prev. work] 22.21.}}
**III.2** **initially,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 58.5, +others}}; **finally,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 60.6, +others}}

1856:
** ἐξωθ-έω**,
_aorist 1_ ἐξέωσα (see. below 11), **thrust out, force out,** ἐκ δ᾽ ὦσε γλήνην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.494, compare 17.618}}; even by pulling, **wrench out,** ἐκ δ᾽ ἄρα οἱ μηροῦ δόρυ μείλινον ὦσε θύραζε {{Refs|5.694}}; **displace,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 46 (_passive_)}}; **expel, eject, banish,** γῆς τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1296; πάτρας [prev. work] 1330}}; **put away** a wife, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PSI” 1.41.16 (4th c.AD)}}; **thrust back,** τοὺς δίκῃ νικῶντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1248}}; **drive,** τοὺς Αακεδαιμονίους ἐς τὰς ἁμάξας {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.72; πλοῖον εἰς αἰγιαλόν NT.Act.27.39, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.60c; τὴν πόλιν εἰς χαλεπόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 12; ἐ. εἰς ἅπαν ἀπὸ τῆς ὄχθης 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.15.4; ἐ. νόμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Ag.” “Gracch.” 5}}:—_passive_, ἐξωθέεσθαι ἐκ τῆς χώρης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.13, compare 5.124; μάχῃ [prev. author] 6.83; πατρίδος ἐξωθούμενος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 428}}; ἐξωσθήσομαι εἰπεῖν **shall be debarred** from.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.61.}}
**2** ἐ. γλώσσας ὀδύναν **put forth** painful words, **break forth into** cruel words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1142 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **drive out of the sea, drive on shore,** τὰς ἄλλας [ναῦς] ἐξέωσαν πρὸς τὴν γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, compare 8.104; ἐς τὴν γῆν [prev. author] 7.52}}:—_passive_, πνεύμασιν ἐξωσθέντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 279 (compare ἐξώστης 11): _metaphorically_, ἐξωσθῆναι τῇ ὥρᾳ ἐς χειμῶνα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}. (Late _infinitive_ ἐξεοῦν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 59.4 “Intr.”}}, _present_ _indicative_ _passive_ ἐξεοῦται {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.2.24.6, formed from ἐξέωσα.)}}

1858:
** ἑορτάζω**,
in _Ionic dialect_Prose **ὁρτάζω·** _imperfect_ ἑώρταζον (with irregular augment) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.40, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.19.4}}: _future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 16, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.18, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἑώρτασα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.34, etc.}}; _infinitive_ ἑορτάσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1079, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 458a}}; compare διεορτάζω: (ἑορτή):—**keep festival** or **holiday,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.60, 122, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1458, etc.}}; ἑορτὰς ἑ. **celebrate** festivals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.2; ἡμέρας τέτταρας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 42; τὴν γενέθλιόν τινος “OGI” 493.26 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD)}}; ἑ. τῷ θεῷ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 23.}}
**II** **celebrate as a festival,** νίκην ἑ. **celebrate** it **by a festival,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.349f, compare “Ant.” 56}}; at Rome, **celebrate by a triumph,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 51.21.}}

1859:
** ἑορτ-ή**,
in _Ionic dialect_ Prose **ὁρτή** (so {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 726.21 (Milet., 5th c.BC)}}, probably in Ion Trag.21, but ἑορτή {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 725.12 (Milet., 6th c.BC))}}, ἡ, **feast, festival, holiday,** ἐπεὶ καὶ πᾶσιν ἑ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.156; ἑ. τοῖο θεοῖο 21.258; ἐούσης ὁρτῆς τῇ Ἥρῃ τοῖσι Ἀργείοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31}}; ὁρτὴν ἄγειν keep **a feast,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 147, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.5, etc.; ἄξεις τότ᾽ ἀμελιτῖτιν ὁρτὴν ἐξ ὁρτῆς 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.85; ὁρτὴν ποιευμένους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.150; ὁρτὴν ἀνάγειν [prev. author] 2.40, 48, +others; ἑορτὰς ἑορτάσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.2; ἑορτὴν τῇ θεῷ ποιεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15; ἡ τῶν Παναθηναίων ἑ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.35: _metaphorically_, οἵας ἑορτῆς ἔστ᾽ ἀπόπτυστοι θεοῖς στέργηθρ᾽ ἔχουσαι, of the Eumenides, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 191; ἑορτὴ ὄψεως 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 13.1.}}
**2** generally, **holiday-making, amusement, pastime,** παιδιᾶς καὶ ἑορτῆς χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276b, etc.; so ἑορτὴν ἡγεῖσθαι τὸ τὰ δέοντα πρᾶξαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70.}}
**3** proverbial, κατόπιν ἑορτῆς ἥκειν to have come the day after **the fair,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447a}}; ἀεργοῖς αἰὲν ἑορτά every day's **a holiday** to those who don't work, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.26, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.17; ἄγουσιν ἑ. οἱ κλέπται 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**4** **assembled multitude at a festival,** ὄχλος καὶ ἑ. καὶ στρατὸς καὶ πλῆθος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.12.}}

1860:
** ἐπαγγ-ελία**, ἡ,
**command, summons,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.38.2.}}
**b** **announcement, notice,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1235.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; τοῦ ἀγῶνος {{LXX|“SIG” 561.9 (from Chalcis), probably in LXX.1Mac.10.15}}; variant in {{NT|NT.1John.1.5.}}
**2** as law-term, ἐ. (i.e. δοκιμασίας) **summons to attend** a δοκιμασία τῶν ῥητόρων (see. ἐπαγγέλλω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3), ἐ. τινὶ ἐπαγγέλλειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.64, compare 81; πρὸς τοὺς θεσμοθέτας ἔσθ᾽ ἡμῖν ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.29}}: generally, **notification, summons,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Sammelb. 4434 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** **offer, promise, profession, undertaking,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.14; τὰς ὑπερβολὰς τῶν ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1164a29, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.20; ἐπαγγελίας ποιεῖσθαί τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.72.6}}; ἐν ἐν ἐπαγγελίᾳ καταλιπών having left it as **a promise,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.28.1}}; τὴν ἐ. ἐπὶ τέλος ἀγαγεῖν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. passage],compare “SIG” 577.11 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; ὤμων ἐπαγγελίᾳ **the promise** of his shoulders, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.4}}; ἐξ ἐ., ={ἐπαγγειλάμενος}, {{Refs|“BCH” 11.12 (from Lagina)}}; ἐ. ποιησάμενος ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων {{Refs|“Michel” 473.10 (from Mylasa)}}; ἐβεβαίωσεν τὴν ἐ. {{Refs|“Inscription Prien.” 123.9, compare “GDI” 3624a34 (from Cos).}}
**4** **indication,** τοῦ ἐσομένου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 205.13.}}
**5** _plural_, **canvassing,** = Latin _ambitus,_ probably _falsa lectio_ for{παρ-}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.276d.}}
**6** ={ἐπάγγελμα} 2, **subject** of a treatise, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Libr.Propr.Prooem.”}}
**7** the **curative property claimed** for prescriptions or drugs, ταῖς τῶν φαρμάκων ἐ. their **advertised properties,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.5.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.504, +others}}; ἐ. ἐπιτηδεύματος **public exercise** of a profession, Men.Prot.p.1D.

1861:
** ἐπαγγ-έλλω**,
_aorist_ _passive_ **-ηγγέλθην**{{Refs|“IG” 12.188.25, -ηγγέλην [prev. work] 12.76.19}}—**tell, proclaim, announce,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.775, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1049 (Lyric poetry); τινὶ ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36; τῷ δήμῳ ὑπέρ τινος ὅτι.. “Inscription Prien.” 5.17 (4th c.BC)}}; especially **proclaim by authority, notify publicly,** ἐ. [τὴν ἐκεχειρίαν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49; ἐ. πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 702d}}:— _passive_, **to be proclaimed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae [prev. cited], etc.; μὴ ἐπηγγέλαι πω τὰς σπονδάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49, compare 8.10}}; βουλῆς -θείσης a meeting **having been summoned,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.29}}:—_middle_, **cause proclamation to be made,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.ζ.}}
**2** **give orders, command,** _absolutely_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.70}}: c._accusative_ et _infinitive_, **give orders that..,** ἐπαγγείλας τοὺς Αακεδαιμονίους παρεῖναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 77, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.56}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, **order** one to do, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.7, etc.:}} with _accusative_ _of things_, στρατιὰν ἐς τοὺς ξυμμάχους ἐ. **send** them **orders** to furnish their contingents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.17; κατὰ πόλεις τεσσαράκοντα νεῶν πλῆθος ἐ. [prev. author] 3.16: _absolutely_, βοηθεῖν.. καθ᾽ ὅ τι ἂν -ωσιν αἱ πόλεις Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47}}:—_middle_, ἐπαγγέλλεσθαί τινι ἑτοιμάζειν στρατιήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.1, compare 4.200; ἐ. τινί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1185 (Lyric poetry); ἐ. τισί ὅκως ἂν ἀπέλθοιεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.98}}:—_passive_, τὸ ἐπαγγελλόμενον {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.55.}}
**3** as law-term, properly δοκιμασίαν ἐ. **denounce and summon to** a δοκιμασία τῶν ῥητόρων one who, having incurred ἀτιμία, yet takes part in public affairs (see. ἐπαγγελία [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2), ἐπήγγειλα αὐτῷ τὴν δοκιμασίαν ταυτηνί 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.2, compare [prev. work]32; πρὸς τοὺς θεσμοθέτας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.23 (but ἐπηγγέλθη αὐτοῖς ὅτι ἐπεξίοιμι is _falsa lectio_ for{ἀπ}-in 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.11).}}]
**4** **promise, offer,** ξείνοις δεῖπνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.31; θεοῖς εὐχάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 213}}:—more frequently in _middle_, **promise unasked** (opposed to ὑπισχνέομαι) or **offer of one's free will,** ἐ. τι ἐς τὴν δωρεὴν τοῖσι ἀδελφεοῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.135; ἐ. καταγωγὴν καὶ ξείνιά τινι [prev. author] 6.35; παίδων.. ἐ. γονάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 721}}; ἁπηγγελλόμην what I **was proposing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1018, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.15; ἐ. τάδε, ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9}}: with _infinitive_, **promise** or **offer,** ξυμπολεμεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88}}; διαθήκας ἀποφαίνειν (-φανεῖν Dobree) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.15; ἐ. τῇ βουλῇ μηνύσειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.15; τισὶν τριήρεις ἔχων ἐκπλεύσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.4, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.132, etc.; τινὶ ὥστε βοηθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.86}}; ἐ. ὅ τι χρὴ δρᾶν **offering** (to do) what in justice he ought to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 915a.}}
**5** _middle_, **profess, make profession of,** with _accusative_, ἀρετήν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.7; θεοσέβειαν NT.1Tim.2.10}}; especially of Sophists, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 273e; τί ἐστιν ὃ ἐ. τε καὶ διδάσκει [prev. author] “Grg.” 447c; τοῦτό ἐστι τὸ ἐπάγγελμα ὃ ἐπαγγέλλομαι [prev. author] “Prt.” 319a; ἐπαγγελλόμενος πάντα.. οὐδὲν ἐπιτελεῖ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1164a5}}; [γνῶσιν] {{NT|NT.1Tim.6.21}}: with _infinitive_, ἐ. ἀποκρίνεσθαι ὅ τι ἄν τίς σε ἐρωτᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447d; ἐ. οἷός τε εἶναι ποιῆσαί τι [prev. author] “La.” 186c, “Thg.” 127e; ταῦτα ἐπαγγέλλεται δεινὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.41; οἱ σοφισταὶ ἐ. διδάσκειν τινά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180b35; παιδεύειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.41}}; and _absolutely_, **profess** an art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 518b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 172a32.}}
**6** **demand, require,** conjecture in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.65}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.193}}; but, **ask a favour,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 41.}}

1862:
** ἐπάγγ-ελμα**, ατος, τό,
**promise, profession,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.178 (_plural_); τὸ Πρωταγόρου ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1402a25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319a; ὑπὸ τοῦ μεγέθους τοῦ ἐ. οὐδὲν θαυμαστὸν ἀπιστεῖν [prev. author] “Euthd.” 274a: _plural_, 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus cited in 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.88 S}}.; ἐπαγγέλματι, opposed to κατ᾽ ἀλήθειαν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.182.}}
**2** **subject** of a treatise, that which it **purports** to contain, τὸ ἐ. τοῦ λόγου {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 33; τὸ ἐ. τοῦ συγγράμματος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tact.Praef.” 7.}}
**3** ={ἐπαγγελία} 7, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Crito Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.878, [prev. author] cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.16.}}
**4** **art, profession,** τὸ ἐ. τῆς ἀοτοποιΐας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.2.}}

1863:
** ἐπάγω** [ᾰ],
**bring on,** οἷον ἐπ᾽ ἦμαρ ἄγῃσι πατήρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.137; ἐ. πῆμά τινι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 242; νύκτ᾽ [prev. author] “Th.” 176}}; ἐλεύθερον ἦμαρ Bacisap.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77; ἄτην ἐπ᾽ ἄτῃ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 404 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1189 (Lyric poetry); κινδύνους τινί 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.3; πόλεμον ἐπὶ τὰς Θήβας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.140; νόσους γῆράς τε ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 33a; πάθος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 3.}}
**2** **set on, urge on,** as hunters do dogs, ἐπάγοντες ἐπῇσαν (i.e. κύνας) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.445, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.19:}} — in _middle_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.25.}}
**2.b** **lead on** an army **against** the enemy, Ἄρη τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 85 (Lyric poetry); τὴν στρατιήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, compare 7.165; τὸ δεξιὸν κέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 353; στρατόπεδον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.69; τινὰ ἐπί τινα [prev. author] 8.46}}: _intransitive_, **march against,** τισί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.29.2: _absolutely_, uncertain in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 21: _metaphorically_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 44 (nisi to be read ἐπῇττε).}}
**2.c** **quicken** the pace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 25, “Nu.” 390, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 420d; θάττονα ῥυθμὸν ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.22.}}
**3** **lead on by persuasion, influence,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.392, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107; ἐλπὶς ἥ σ᾽ ἐπήγαγεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1032}}: with _infinitive_, **induce** one to do,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 260, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.63}}:—_passive_, οἷς ἐπαχθέντες ὑμεῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.10 (codex S).}}
**4** **bring in, invite** as aiders or allies, τὸν Πέρσην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.1, compare 8.112}}; τὸν Π. ἐπὶ τοὺς Ἕλληνας Epist. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.7; Μήδους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 365 (see. below 11.2.}}
**5** **bring to** a place, **bring in,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 378, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 905; ἅμαξαι.. τοὺς λίθους ἐπῆγον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93}}:—_middle_, **draw in** nourishment, of roots, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.1.9}}:— _passive_, τροφὰ ἐπάγεται τῷ σώματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 102b.}}
**6** **bring in, supply,** ἐπιτήδεια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.60; τὰ ἐκ τῶν διωρύχων ἐ. νάματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118e; λίμνην.. εἰς τὴν ἅλμην 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 5.12: _metaphorically_, ἐπάγει ἡ ψυχὴ τὸ ἓν ἄλλῳ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.1.}}
**7** **lay on** or **apply** to one, ἐ. κέντρον πώλοις, of a charioteer, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1194; ἐ. πληγὴν ἐπί τινα LXX.Isa.10.24}}; ἐ. ζημίαν, ={ἐπιτιθέναι}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 11}}; ἔπαγε τὴν γνάθον **lay** your jaws **to it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 370}}; ἐ. τὴν διάνοιάν τινι **apply** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 1.}}
**8** **bring forward,** ἐ. ψῆφον τοῖς ξυμμάχοις **propose** a vote to them, like{ἐπιψηφίζειν} ἐς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.125, compare 87}}; ψῆφος ἐπῆκτό τινι περὶ φυγῆς **against** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.57, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.28; ἐ. ὅρκον τισί 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.14.4, compare “IG” 9(1).334.13 (_Locrian dialect_)}}; also ἐ. δίκην, γραφήν τινι, **bring** a suit **against** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 881e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.150; γραφάς, εὐθύνας, εἰσαγγελίας [prev. work]249; λεγέτω πρότερος ὁ ἐπάγων τὰν δίκαν “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 1 A 10; ἐ. αἰτίαν τινί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.141; αἰτίαν ἐπήγαγέ μοι φόνου ψευδῆ [prev. author] 21.110, compare114.}}
**8.b** **introduce** a person before the assembly, {{Refs|“IG” 12(7).389.5, “BCH” 50.251, etc.}}
**9** **bring in over and above,** παροψώνημα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1446; τῷ λόγῳ τὸ ἔργον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 8:}} —_passive_, τὸ ἐπαγόμενον φωνῆεν the vowel **which follows,** {{Refs|EM 176.55}}; ὁ ἐ. ἀγών **extraordinary**, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3491 (from Thyatira).}}
**9.b** **intercalate** days in the year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.50}}; αἱ ἐπαγόμεναι, with or without ἡμέραι, **intercalated** days,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 13, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.355e, “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 134 H., “PStrassb.” 91.6, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 20.26, 36.9, etc.}}
**10** in instruction or argument, **lead on,** τινὰς ἐπὶ τὰ μήπω γιγνωσκόμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 278a}}:—_passive_, ἐπαχθέντων αὐτῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.23 M.}}
**10.b** especially in the Logic of Aristotle, **teach** or **convince by induction,** ἐπάγοντα ἀπὸ τῶν καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ἐπὶ τὸ καθόλου καὶ τῶν γνωρίμων ἐπὶ τὰ ἄγνωστα {{Refs|“Top.” 156a4}}:—_passive_, ἐπαχθῆναι μὴ ἔχοντας αἴσθησιν ἀδύνατον {{Refs|“APo.” 81b5, compare71a21,24: _absolutely_, συλλογιζόμενον ἢ ἐπάγοντα}} by syllogism or **by induction, **{{Refs|“Rh.” 1356b8,compare “Top.” 157a21, +others; οὐδ᾽ ὁ ἐπάγων ἀποδείκνυσιν “APo.” 91b15.}}
**10.c** also ἐ. τὸ καθόλου **bring forward, advance**: hence, **infer** the general principle, τῇ καθ᾽ ἕκαστα ἐπὶ τῶν ὁμοίων ἐπαγωγῇ ἐ. τὸ καθόλου {{Refs|“Top.” 108b11, compare “SE” 174a34}}; so later, **adduce the argument,** ὅτι.. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus}}**inSE**6.2; **conclude, infer,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.8.9.}}
**11** ἐ. τὴν κοιλίαν **move** the bowels, variant for{ὑπ-}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.157.}}
**II** _middle_, **bring to oneself, procure** or **provide for oneself,** ἐκ θαλάσσης ὧν δέονται ἐπάξονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, compare 6.99: _metaphorically_, Ἅιδα φεῦξιν ἐ.}} **devise, invent** a means of shunning death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 362 (Lyric poetry); τὴν τῶν ξυμμάχων δούλωσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.10; τῶν.. κακῶν ἐ. λήθην 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 467.}}
**II.2** of persons, **bring into** one's country, **bring in** or **introduce** as allies (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|above 1.4), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.108, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, 2.68, 4.64, +others; οἰκιστὴν ἐ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.34, compare 5.67; ἐπιϝοίκους ἐ. “Berl.Sitsb.” 1927.8 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC).}}
**II.3** μάρτυρας ποιητὰς ἐ. **call** them **in** as witnesses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 364c, compare “Lg.” 823a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 995a8}}; ἐ. ποιητὰς ἐν τοῖς λόγοις **introduce** by way of quotation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 347e; τὸν Ἡσίοδον μάρτυρα [prev. author] “Ly.” 215c}}; ἐ. μαρτύρια **adduce** testimonies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.34; εἰκόνας ἐ. [prev. author] “Oec.” 17.15; ὅρκον ἐ. πάντα τὰ ζῷα 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.16.}}
**II.4** **bring upon oneself,** νύκτα ἐν μεσημβρίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 897d; φθόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 32; συμφορὰν ἐμαυτῷ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.19; αὐθαίρετον αὑτοῖς δουλείαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.259; πράγματα [prev. author] 54.1; ἑαυτοῖς δεστότην ἐ. τὸν νόμον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 492b; μητρυιὰν ἐ. κατὰ τῶν ἰδίων τέκνων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.12.}}
**II.5** **bring with one,** προῖκα πολλήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.22.102.}}
**II.6** **bring over to oneself, win over,** τὸ πλῆθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45; τινὰ εἰς εὔνοιαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.14.4: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, ἐ. τινὰς ξυγχωρῆσαι **induce** them to concede, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.41.}}
**II.7** **put in place,** λίθον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Princeton Exp.Inscription” 1175 (3rd c.AD).}}

1864:
** ἐπᾰγωνίζομαι**,
**contend with,** τινί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 23}}; **continue to attack,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator “Epistulae” 2.2.}}
**2** with _dative_ _of things_, **contend in,** εὐνοίᾳ {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).860.19 (from Tenos)}}; **contend for,** τῇ πίστει {{NT|NT.Jude.3}}: **lay stress on,** ἐ. τῷ λόγῳ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.246; τεκμηρίοις ἐ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 8; ἐ. τῇ λέξει τὰ ἰσοδυναμοῦντα παρατιθείς 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “τέχναι ῥητορικαί” 1p.500S.: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 3.93}}; **exertoneself,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1343.16.}}
**3** **contend again,** in games, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 7.6.}}
**3.b** **speak after** a person, **follow** him, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.25.7; ἐ. τῷ λόγῳ 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Argumenta Orationum Demosthenicarum” 22.}}

1867:
** ἐπαιν-έω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **ἐπαίνημι** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.19}}; _Laconian dialect_ **ἐπαινίω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 198:}}_imperfect_ ἐπῄνεον Il {{Refs|3.461 (tmesis)}}: _future_ **-έσω**{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.29, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1057, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 465 (Lyric poetry), “Heracl.” [300], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 214e (uncertain reading), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.16, 5.5.8}}: but more frequently **-έσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1195 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 199a, “R.” 379e, 383a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.31, etc.}} _poetry_ **-ήσω** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 93 codices, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.69}}: _aorist 1_ ἐπῄνεσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 536, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86, etc. (see. below); poetry (not Trag.) ἐπῄνησα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.335, 18.312, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 876, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.168, 189}}: also _Aeolic dialect_ prose, {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 622.21, 623.31, 636.17 (but -έσαι 623.34)}}: _perfect_ ἐπῄνεκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.207,261, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 307a, etc.}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐπῃνησάμην {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 16.200c}}; **-εσάμην** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 147}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἐπαινεθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474d; later ἐπαινηθήσομαι 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 4.4}} codices: _aorist_ ἐπῃνέθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.146, etc.; but ἐπῃνήθην “SIG” 708.20 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC)}}: _perfect_ ἐπῄνημαι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.233: ={αἰνέω} (for which it is regularly used in _Attic dialect_)}}]:—**approve, applaud, commend,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly _absolutely_, ἐπὶ δ᾽ ᾔνεον ἄλλοι Ἀχαιοί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.461, etc.: with _accusative_ _of things_, μῦθον ἐπαινήσαντες Ὀδυσσῆος 2.335; μῦθον ἐ. πρεσβυτέροισι “hymnus ad Mercurium” 457; σύνθεσιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.168}}: with _dative_ _person_, **agree with, side with,** Ἕκτορι μὲν γὰρ ἐπῄνησαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.312}}: _absolutely_, **assent, agree,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1616}}; ἐπαινεσάντων δ᾽ αὐτῶν on their **assent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.65.}}
**2** **praise, commend** in any way (the usually sense in _Attic dialect_ and Trag.), τινά or τι, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 37 A, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34, 6.130; τὸ λίαν ἧσσον ἐ. τοῦ μηδὲν ἄγαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 264}}; ἐ. τινά τι **commend** one **for** a thing, but in this case the thing is always a _neuter_ _pronoun_ or _adjective_, τὰ μέν σ᾽ ἐπαινῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 342 (compare 111); πάντ᾽ ἔχω σ᾽ ἐπαινέσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1381, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222a; in 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.22}} ἐπαινεῖσθαι ταῖς ζητήσεσιν, [ἐπί] should be read; ἐπί τινι.. καὶ διότι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 44.17 (2nd c.BC)}}; εἴς τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.111a; κατά τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.37; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 145b; also ἐ. τινά τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 42 (but ἐ. τί τινος}} **praise** something in some one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361d): c._accusative_ _cognate_, ἔπαινον ἐ. [prev. author] “La.” 181b}}: with _participle_, ἐπαινέσεσθαί τινα ἀνασχόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.73; ἐ. τινὰ ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471d}}; ἐ. τινὰ πρός τινα **praise** one man to another, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 501c}}; especially **compliment publicly,** [Βρασίδας] πρῶτος τῶν κατὰ τὸν πόλεμον ἐπῃνέθη ἐν Σπάρτῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.25}}; frequently in honorary Inscrr.,{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|compare “IG” 22.102, “Inscription Prien.” [same place], etc.: with _dative_ _person_, τῷ δήμῳ τῷ Σαμίων “IG” 12.101.2, compare “SIG” 604.11 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** of things, [πολιτεία] ὑπὸ πάντων -ουμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.118, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289a1; νόμοι -ούμενοι [prev. author] “Rh.” 1375b24}}; **approve,** πόλις ἄλλως ἄλλοτ᾽ ἐ. τὰ δίκαια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1077.}}
**4** **agree to** or **undertake to do,** ῥώμην μ᾽ ἐπαινῶ λαμβάνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 553; ἐ. εἰς τὸ λοιπόν “PTeb.” 8.18 (3rd c.BC).}}
**5** _aorist_ ἐπῄνεσα in _Attic dialect_ in _present_ sense, ἐπῄνεσ᾽ ἔργον I **commend** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 536}}: _absolutely_, **well done!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 282, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 485, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1095, “Med.” 707.}}
**II** ={παραινέω}, **recommend, advise,** τοιούσδ᾽ ἐπαινεῖς δῆτα δὺ κτᾶσθαι φίλου; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1360, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 596, “Supp.” 996}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, ὑμῖν δ᾽ ἐπαινῶ γλῶσσαν εὔφημον φέρειν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 581}}; σιγᾶν ἐπῄνεσ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(compare 1.5) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1322, compare “OC” 665.}}
**III** as a civil form of declining an offer or invitation, I **thank you,** I **am much obliged,** κάλλιστ᾽, ἐπαινῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 508 (ubi see Scholia), compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 342}}; so ἐ. τὴν κλῆσιν **decline** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.7, compare “An.” 7.7.52.}}
**IV** of Rhapsodists, **recite, declaim publicly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 536d, 541e.}}

1868:
** ἔπαιν-ος**, ὁ,
**approval, praise, commendation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 4.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 181; ἔ. ἔχειν πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96; πολλῷ ἐχρᾶτο τῷ ἐ. [prev. author] 3.3}}: frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἐπαίνου τυχεῖν ἔκτινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 665, etc.}}; κλεινὴ καὶ ἔπαινον ἔχουσα meriting **praise,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 817; ἔπαινον ἐπαινεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181b}}: _plural_, **praises,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 720, “El.” 976, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.33; τιμαὶ.. καὶ ἔ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516c, etc.}}
**2** **complimentary address, panegyric** (but distinguished from ἐγκώμιον, as the general from the particular, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1219b15, “Rh.” 1367b27); ἔ. ποιεῖσθαι περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260c; λόγον εἰπεῖν ἔπαινον Ἔρωτος [prev. author] “Smp.” 177d; συντιθεὶς λόγον ἔ. κατά τινος [prev. author] “Phdr.” 260b; οἱ κατὰ Δημοσθένους ἔ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.50; εἴς τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 947c; ὑπέρ τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.1.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.22, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.57.}}

1869:
** ἐπαίρω**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ **ἐπᾰείρω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204 and always in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}: _future_ ἐπᾱρῶ (_contraction_ from ᾰερ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 125 (_anapaest meter_), “Supp.” 581 (probable reading), X “Mem.” 3.6.2}}: _aorist_ ἐπῆρα, _participle_ ἐπάρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἐπῆρκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 13, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 8.114b:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐπήρθην, _participle_ ἐπαρθείς: **lift up and set on,** [αὐτὸν] ἀμαξάων ἐπάειρραν **lifted and set** him **upon**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.426; ὀβελοὺς.. κρατευτάων ἐπάειρας 9.214.}}
**2** **lift, raise,** κεφαλὴν ἐπαείρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|10.80; καί μ᾽ ἔπαιρε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 889; ἐπαίρων βλέφαρα [prev. author] “OT” 1276 codices; ἐπάειρε δέρην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 99 (_anapaest meter_); ἔπαιρε σαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 996; σεμνῶς ἐπηρκὼς τὰς ὀφρῦς 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus [same place]}}; πάντες ἐπῆραν (i.e. τὴν χεῖρα) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1109.24; οὐδεὶς ἐπῆρε “IG” 3.1132; ἐπάρας τὴν φωνήν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.291}}; ἐπαιρόμενα ἱστία, opposed to ὑφιέμενα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 3:}} —_middle_, με τεῷ ἐπαείραο μαζῷ **didstliftand put** me **to** thy breast, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.734}}; [λόγχην] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1484; ὅπλ᾽ ἐπαίρεσθαι θεῷ [prev. author] “Ba.” 789; ἱστούς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.61.7; βακτηρίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.185b: _metaphorically_, τί.. στάσιν γλώσσης ἐπήρασθ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 635; πολλοὺς καὶ θρασεῖς τῇ πόλει λόγους ἐπαιρόμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.222; κοινὸν ἡ πόλις ἐπήρατο πένθος 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34.17.}}
**3** **exalt, magnify,** ἐπαείρειν Αοκρῶν ματέρ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.20; ἐπαρεῖς τὸν πατρῷον οἶκον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.2.}}
**4** _intransitive_, **lift up one's leg** or **rise up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.162}}; **rise from table,** {{Refs|Euangelus Comicus 1.10.}}
**5** _passive_, **swell up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὑγρῶν χρήσιος” 2, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.264, 18(2).119}}; ἐπῆρται τοῦτό γε, in mal. _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 937; ὁ καυλὸς ἐπαίρεται “Hippiatrica” 54.}}
**6** Grammars, ἐ. τὴν προσῳδίαν **make** the accent **acute,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.636.}}
**II** **stir up, excite,** πολλά τέ μιν καὶ μεγάλα τὰ ἐπαείροντα.. ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204; τίς σ᾽ ἐπῆρε δαιμόνω; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1328; πέρα τοῦ καιροῦ τοὺς ἑτέρους ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 16.23; ἐ. θυμόν τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 125; τοῦτό σε ψυχὴν ἐπαίρει [prev. author] “Heracl.” 173; ἑαυτὸν ἐπίτινι 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 64; ἵππον}}**urge on,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 1.13c}}; **induce, persuade** to do, c._infinitive_, εἰρωτᾶν εἰ οὔτι ἐπαισχύνεται ἐπάρας Κροῖσον στρατεύεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.108, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.192; ἥτις με γῆμ᾽ ἐπῆρε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 42, compare “Ra.” 1041; ἐ. τινὰ ὥστε.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 581}}; ὅστις μ᾽ ἐπάρας ἔργον (i.e. πρᾶξαι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 286}}:—_passive_, **to be roused, led on, excited,** τῷ μαντηΐῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, compare 5.91; τοῖσι δωρήμασι [prev. author] 7.38; τοῖς τῆς πόλεως κακοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.37; ὑπὸ τῆς τύχης 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.10}}; πλούτῳ, τιμῇ, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 434b, 608b; ὑπὸ λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1448; τῇ ἐλπίδι ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.21; τοῖς λόγοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.121; δεινότητι καὶ ξυνέσεως ἀγῶνι [prev. author] 3.37 (so τὸ ἐπαιρόμενον τοῦ λόγου τῇ δεινότητι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 25); ὑπὸ μεγάλου μισθοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13; ἐ. ἐς τὸ νεωτερίζειν [prev. author] 4.108; ἐπὶ τὴν βασιλείαν LXX.3Ki.12. [24]}}: with _infinitive_, ἐπήρθην γράψαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.10}}; τῷ or τὸ λέγειν (uncertain reading) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232a (but ναυτικῷ προύχειν -όμενοι}} **flattering themselves** that they were superior.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25)}}: _absolutely_, **to be excited, on tiptoe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 810; and so Ἑλλὰς τῇ ὁρμῇ ἐπῆρται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11.}}
**II.2** _passive_, also, **to be elated at** a thing, εὐδαιμονίῃ μεγάλῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.81; ψυχρῇ νίκῃ [prev. author] 9.49, compare 1.212, 4.130; ἐπὶ πλούτῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.25; πρός τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.11, 8.2; ἐκ τοῦ γεγονότος προτερήματος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.4: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18.}}

1870:
** ἐπαισχ-ύνομαι**,
_future_ -αισχυνθήσομαι, **to be ashamed at** or **of,** τῷ οὐνόματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.143}}; τινά or τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 247c}}: with _infinitive_, **to be ashamed** to do, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1373}}: with _participle_, **to be ashamed of** doing or having done a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1307, “Ph.” 929, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **feel shame, show a sense of shame,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 573b, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 625.}}
**II** late in _active_, **make ugly, mar,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 20.61, 42.421.}}

1871:
** ἐπαιτ-έω**,
**ask besides,** εἰ καί νύ κεν.. ἄλλο μεῖζον ἐπαιτήσειας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.593; ὧν ἐπαιτεῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1416}}: _absolutely_, **ask for more,** φαγὼν ἔτ᾽ ἐπῄτεον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.412e}}:—so in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1124.}}
**2** **beg as a mendicant,** ἄλλους ἐ. τὸν καθ᾽ ἡμέραν βίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1364, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 715: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 68.30, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 35.}}
**3** simply, **demand,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 26.13 (2nd c.BC, _passive_), etc.}}

1872:
** ἐπᾰκολουθ-έω**,
**follow close upon, follow after, pursue,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1328, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23c, +others}}; **move with,** τῷ ἄλλῳ σώματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 16; ἐ. ἡ χεὶρ τοῦ νεκροῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.8.}}
**2** **pursue** as an enemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.128, 5.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” [4.1.1], etc.}}
**3** **attend to, follow** mentally, **understand,** τῷ λόγῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107b; τοῖς λεγομένοις [prev. author] “Lg.” 861c; αὐτοῖς λέγουσι [prev. author] “Sph.” 243a; κάλλιστ᾽ ἐπακολουθεῖς [prev. author] “Lg.” 963a, etc.}}
**4** **attend to, follow,** i.e. **obey** or **comply with,** ταῖς τῶν συμμάχων γνώμαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.90; τοῖς πάθεσι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.18}}; αὐτῶν τῇ προαιρέσει Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.167}}; ταῖς τῶν ποιητῶν βλασφημίαις ἐ. **follow** them (as authorities), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.38: with _dative_ _person_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1096b7.}}
**5** **attend to,** i.e. **execute,** a task, τῷ πραττομένῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370c}}; **wait upon,** of bees, τοῖς βασιλεῦσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 760b15.}}
**6** **supervise, attend to,** τῇ ἐγχύσει τοῦ γλεύκους {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.136 (3rd c.BC),compare “PAmh.” 2.40.24 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1024.33 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**7** **concur,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PFay. 24.19 (2nd c.AD).}}
**8** **verify, check,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 10.8 (3rd c.BC), “PGen.” 22.1 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **accompany, result, accrue,** τινί {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.59 W., +others: βλάβος, ζημία ἐ., “PRyl.” 126.19 (1st c.AD), “BGU” 3.14 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** τὰ ἐπακολουθοῦντα σημεῖα **confirmatory, authenticating** signs {{NT|compare(1.7), NT.Mark.16.20.}}
**II.3** of the offspring of cattle, πρόβατα σὺν τοῖς -οῦσι ἄρνασι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 245.11 (1st c.AD), compare 244.9 (1st c.AD).}}

1873:
** ἐπᾰκού-ω**,
_future_ ἐπακούσομαι{{LXX|LXX.Gen.30.33}}, later ἐπακούσω {{Refs|“Psalms of Solomon [1st c.BC]” 18.3}}:—**hear,** with _accusative_ _of things_, ὂς πάντ᾽ ἐφορᾷς καὶ πάντ᾽ ἐπακούεις, of the Sun, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.277, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.109}}; proverbial, ὁπποῖόν κ᾽ εἴπῃσθα ἔπος, τοῖόν κ᾽ ἐπακούσαις as thou speakest, so **wilt** thou **be answered,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.250; φωνὴν ἐ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 448; χρησμόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1080}}: with _accusative_ _of things_ et _genitive_ _person_, ἔπος ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.98: with _genitive_ _of things_, εὐχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 263; τῆς φωνῆς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.70: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.53, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.98, etc.}}
**2** **overhear,** μή τις τῶν ἀμυήτων ἐπακούσῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 628.}}
**3** **hear about, hear tell of,** μόχθων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 165 (Lyric poetry)}}; with _participle_, οἷον γᾶς Ἀσίας οὐκ ἐπακούω.. βλαστὸν φύτευμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 695 (Lyric poetry); τινά τι δρῶντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729b.}}
**4** **give ear, listen,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 725 (_anapaest meter_): with _genitive_ _person_, ἐμοῦ 'πάκουσον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 708, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317d}}; ἐ. μοι 'pray **attend**', {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 227c}}: especially of **giving ear** to one who prays, of God, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Isa.49.8, “UPZ” 78.24 (2nd c.BC)}}; or to advice, commands, etc., i.e. **obey,** βουλῆς {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.143; δίκης 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 275; ἐμῶν μύθων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1417 (_anapaest meter_): with _dative_ _of things_, τῷ κελεύσματι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.141; ταῖς εὐχαῖς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 13.6, cf. LXX.Hos.2.21(23).}}
**5** later, like{ἐπαΐω}, **perceive, understand,** τῶν ᾀδομένων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 64, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 10}} (or, **hear distinctly**).
**6** ἐπακούσεταί μοι ἡ δικαιοσύνη μου **shall answer for** me, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.30.33.}}

1875:
** ἐπάν**,
_conjunction_, later form of ἐπήν (see. ἐπεί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 42.2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 24.10, “IG” 22.1298.18 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.2.9, 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 32, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.4.20, etc.: with _indicative_ in late Greek, ἐπὰν ἑάλω Scholia 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.”}}
**9** [ᾱ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 223.2, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 269.]}}

1876:
Included with: ** ἐπᾰνάγκ-ης**,
used only in _neuter_:
**1** ἐπάναγκές [ἐστι] **it is compulsory, necessary,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 878e, etc.}}; μηδὲν ἐ. ἔστω let there be no **compulsion,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 765a, compare “Smp.” 176e.}}
**2** as _adverb_, **on compulsion,** ἐ. κομῶντες wearing long hair **by fixed custom,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82}}; ἐ. λέγειν, ἐντίθεσθαι, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.24, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.7; ἐ. λαβεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 576; ἐ. βουλὴν ἀθροισάτω “IG” 22.1100.50, etc.; τὰ ἐπάναγκες NT.Act.15.28.}}

1877:
** ἐπαν-άγω** [ᾰγ],
**bring up**: hence,
**1** **stir up, excite,** τὸν θυμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.160.}}
**2** **exalt, elevate,** εἰς ἡρωϊκὴν ἐπανῆκται τάξιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.9.}}
**II** **bring up,** πρὸς τὸ φῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 724a.}}
**II.2** **lead** or **draw back,** τὸ στρατόπεδον ἐς τὴν εὐρυχωρίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.3; ἐ. τὰ δεξιά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.13; τὸν ἄνθρωπον ἐπανήγαγεν ὡς ὑμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.133; σύαγρον εἰς τὴν οἰκίαν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 42 (assuming variant).}}
**II.3** **bring back,** τινὰ εἰς τὸν περὶ τοῦ πράγματος λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 949b; τὸν λόγον ἐπὶ τὴν ὑπόθεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.6.13; ἐ. ἐμαυτὸν ἀπὸ τῶν κακῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 325a}}; εἰς ἐλευθερίαν τὰ πράγματα variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.19}}; **restore,** τὰς αἱρέσεις τῶν ἱερέων εἰς τὸν δῆμον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.37; τὸν οἶκον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.28; τὰ ἱερά [prev. work] 2 (_passive_)}}; τὰ ἀδικήματα εἰς τὰ κοινὰ δικαστήρια ἐ. **refer** them to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 846b, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.62U.; ἐ. τὸ δισταζόμενον εἰς τὸν κανόνα “UPZ” 110.57 (2nd c.BC)}}; but τῷ Δὶ ἐ. **make acknowledgements** to Zeus, {{Refs|[prev. work] 6}}:—_passive_, **to be referred back,** ἐπαναγέσθω πάλιν ἐπὶ τοὺς ἄρχοντας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298b37}}; **to be restored,** ἐπὶ ἀρχὰς καὶ στρατηγίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.15.}}
**II.4** ἐ. ἐπί τι **lead to, entail,** ἐπ᾽ ἀλγοῦν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 26, 30.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **withdraw, retreat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.3}}; **revert,** ἐπὶ τὴν ἀρχήν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.5.9, etc.}}; **recur,** in argument, ὅθεν ἐξέβην {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.226c}}; **return,** ἐπὶ ὕψιστον {{LXX|LXX.Sir.17.26}}; **turn back,** ἀπὸ δικαιοσύνης εἰς ἁμαρτίαν {{Refs|[prev. work] 26.28.}}
**III.2** ἐ. τῷ σώματι **recover one's health,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1 “Praef.”}}
**IV** **put out to sea,** τὸ κέρας ἀπὸ τῆς γῆς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.28: _absolutely_, NT.Luke.5.3}}:—_passive_, **put to sea against,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.98}}; ἐπανάγεσθαι ταῖς ναυσί with one's ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.42: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.194, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.24; ἐπὶ τὴν Χίον [prev. work] 1.6.38}}; **sail up** the Nile, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Pstrassb.” 102.19 (3rd c.BC).}}
**V** _passive_, also, **to be carried to** a place, ἐπαναχθέντας [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.103, where however the variant{ἐπαναχθέντες} (in signification iv) is to be preferred.}}]

1879:
** ἐπανα-παύω**,
**rest upon,** τῇ λαβῇ τοῦ ξίφους τὴν χεῖρα Procop.Gaz.p.170B.,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.56:}} — _middle_ (_future_ -παύσομαι, later -παήσομαι variant in {{NT|NT.Luke.10.6)}}, **rest upon,** ταῖς χερσί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.3.6, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.1.2}}; **rest in** or **upon,** τινί {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.8.12}}; **rest one's hopes on,** νόμῳ {{NT|NT.Rom.2.17}}; **rest content with, rely on,** ταῖς παλαιαῖς ἀποβάσεσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4.65; τῇ ἐφημέρῳ τροφῇ 1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus “περὶ τρόπων” p.194 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; ταῖς διωρισμέναις ἐννοίαις 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 37}}: in Logic, **to be based on,** ὁ δεύτερος συλλογισμὸς ἐ. τῇ ἐννοίᾳ τοῦ ἑνός {{Refs|[prev. work] 321.}}
**II** _middle_, **come to rest,** of a machine, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 24.4: _metaphorically_, ἐ. ἔν τινι}} **come to rest** in, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 8}}; ἐπαναπαύσεται ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν (i.e. τὸν οἶκον) ἡ εἰρήνη ὑμῶν {{LXX+NT|NT.Luke.10.6; ἐπανεπαύσατο ἐπ᾽ αὐτοῖς πνεῦμα LXX.Num.11.26.}}

1880:
** ἐπαν-έρχομαι**,
**go back, return,** ἐκ ποταμοῦ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 23; ἐκ Πειραιέως 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.81, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16}}; ἐς τὴν Κόρινθον{{Refs|[prev. work] 74; θάλασσα-ελθοῦσα ἀπὸ τῆς γῆς [prev. author] 3.89}}; in writing or speaking, **go back** or **return** to a point, ἐκεῖσε δὴ᾽ πάνελθε, πῶς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 256; ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.29; ἐπανελθεῖν ὁπόθεν.. ἐξέβην βούλομαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.211; ἀλλ᾽ ἐκεῖσε ἐπανέρχομαι [prev. work]66; εἰς τὰ γράμματα ταῦτα ἐπανελθεῖν}}**refer to..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 28.5.}}
**b** **recur,** of intermittent fevers, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.412, 16.711.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **return to, recapitulate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 17b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.2, “Ages.” 11.1.}}
**II** **go up, ascend,** εἰς ὄρη {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 4.8.35; δοκέει.. ἐνθεῦτεν γεωμετρίη.. ἐς τὴν Ἑλλάδα ἐπανελθεῖν}}**to have gone up, passed over,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109.}}
**II.2** **rise up** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἐπάνειμι 11), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 17.}}

1881:
** ἐπαν-ίστημι**,
**set up again,** τὰ τείχη [prev. work] 778d.
**2** **make to rise against,** ἄνδρας ἐκ χαράδρας ἐ. τινί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 13}}; **raise in revolt against,** Ἰβηρίαν Ῥωμαίοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 101.}}
**3** **cause to arise,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.654f.}}
**II** _passive_, with _future_ _middle_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62, 1.89)}}, _aorist 2_ and _perfect_ _active_, **stand up after** another or **at** his **word,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, οἱ δ᾽ ἐπανέστησαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.85.}}
**II.b** **rise from bed, rise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 539; ἐπὶ τοῦ καταστρώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.18}}; **rise** to speak, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.2, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.46}}; of buildings, in _perfect_, **to be raised** or **built,** ἢν τοῦτ᾽ ἐπανεστήκῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 554}}: with _genitive_, **rise above,** ἱερῷ -στηκότι τῆς ἀγορᾶς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.50}}; ταῖς -ισταμέναις [ἐκ add. codex unus] τῶν ὑδάτων πομφόλυξιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.75.}}
**II.2** **rise up against, rise in insurrection against,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89, 130, 3.62; τῷ δήμῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.115, etc.; τοῖς πράγμασι 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.19}}: _absolutely_, **rise in insurrection,** opposed to ἀφίσταμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39, +others; οἱ ἐπανεστεῶτες}}**the insurgents,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.63: with}}_infinitive_, ἐάν τις τυραννεῖν ἐπαναστῇ if any one **aim at** tyranny, Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.97}}; in mal. _participle_, ἐ. ἀλλήλοις πώγωνας ἔχουσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 217c; παρθένοις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 15.}}
**II.3** in Medicine texts, of tumours, etc., **rise, swell,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.165; [ὦτα] ἐπανεστηκότα}}**projecting, prorninent,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 691a13}}; λόφος αὐτῶν τῶν πτερῶν ἐ. crest **which sticks up** and is composed of feathers, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 504b10.}}

1883:
** ἐπάνω** [ᾰ],
_adverb_, (ἄνω) **above, on the upper side** or **part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 773, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 514b, etc.}}; with _Article_, ὁ ἐ. πύργος the **upper** tower, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.54, etc.}}
**2** as _preposition_, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.179 (in tmesi, ἐπὶ τοῦ σήματος ἄνω [prev. work] 93), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109d; ἐ. τῆς χώρας “IG” 12(5).872.32 (Tenos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἐ. γεγονότες κακίας having risen **superior to..,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1063c; γίγνεται ἐ. τῆς πληγῆς 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.4.2; ἐ. χρημάτων εἶναι 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.28 (but ἐ. χρημάτων τεταγμένος}} set **over,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 48.5).}}
**3** **before, in front of,** with _genitive_, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.18.2, LXX.2Ki.24.20}}; **in the presence of,** τινός {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 903.14, 20 (4th c.AD).}}
**II** **above,** in a book, ἐν τοῖς ἐ. εἴρηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.1 (interpolated), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1012b6; τὰ ἐ. λεχθέντα 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.8; καθὼς ἐ. γέγραπται “IG” 9(1).694.131 (Corc.), compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3059.3 (from Teos), Polystr.p.22 W.}}
**III** of Time, ἐν τοῖς ἐ. χρόνοις in **former** times, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.42, 18.49; ἐν τῷ ἐ. μηνί “OGI” 764.40 (Pergam.).}}
**IV** of Relationship, πατέρες καὶ τούτων ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.7; οἱ ἐ. πρόγονοι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.7}}; ἐ. ὄντες Εὐρωπαῖοι citizens of Europus **in unbroken descent,** Cumont **Fouilles de Doura-Europos** {{Refs|p.300.}}
**V** in Logic, τὸ ἐ. γένος the genus or species **above,** opposed to τὰ ὑποκάτω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 122a4, 143a21; τὰ ἐ. τοῦ γένους [prev. work] 122a34.}}
**VI** of Number, **above, more,** ἀπὸ εἰκοσαετοῦς καὶ ἐ. {{LXX|LXX.Exo.30.14, +others}}; **above, more than,** ἐ. τριακοσίων δηναρίων {{NT|NT.Mark.14.5; ὤφθη ἐ. πεντακοσίοις NT.1Cor.15.6.}}

1884:
** ἐπαρκ-έω**,
_future_ -έσω (see. below): _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _infinitive_ ἐπαρκέσσαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1161, compare “IG” 5(1).730.18}}:—**to be strong enough for** a thing, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always of cases of danger or injury: 1}}. with _accusative_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, **ward off** something from one, οὐδέ τί οἱ τό γ᾽ ἐπήρκεσε λυγρὸν ὄλεθρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.873; οὔτε τι Τηλέμαχος τό γ᾽ ἐπήρκεσεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.568; οὐδὲν γὰρ αὐτῳ ταῦτ᾽ ἐπαρκέσει τὸ μὴ οὐ πεσεῖν}}**prohibebit quominus..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 918.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **ward off, prevent,** ἐπαρκέσσαι κακότητα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1161}}; σέ τοι μόνον δέδορκα πημονάν (Reiske for ποιμένων) ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 360 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** with _dative_ _person_ only, **help, assist,** {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 871, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.93, NT.1Tim.5.10, etc.: rarely with _accusative_ _person_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 803 (troch.)}}: _absolutely_, τίς ἄρ᾽ ἐπαρκέσε; who **will aid?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 91 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 777.}}
**II** **supply, furnish,** ἄκος δ᾽ οὐδὲν ἐπἤρκεσαν, τὸ μὴ πόλιν.. παθεῖν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1170 (Lyric poetry); ἐ. τινί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 830, LXX.1Mac.11.35, etc.}}; also ἐ. τινὶ τῶν ἑαυτοῦ **impart** to him **a share of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.60}}: with _dative_ _of things_, **supply with,** πέπλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 301.}}
**III** _absolutely_, **to be sufficient, enough,** ὅσσον ἐπαρκεῖ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 5.1 (variant{ἀπαρκεῖ})}}]; ἐπαρκέσει νόμος ὅδ᾽ this law **shall prevail,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 612 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.2** **stand to the credit of,** with _dative_, γενεᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.60.}}

1885:
** ἐπαρχ-ία**, ἡ,
**the government of an** ἔπαρχος, or **the district governed by him,** = Latin _provincia,_ {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.19.2, “SIG” 888.45 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.4.20, 17.3.25 (_plural_), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 37.10, 38.8, +others, NT.Act.23.34, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 4}}; of Carthage, **empire,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phlegon Trallianus Paradoxographus “Miracula” 18.}}
**II** military **'command', force occupying a district,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 96.49 (_plural_).}}

1886:
** ἔπαυλ-ις**, εως, ἡ,
**steading,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111; οὐκ ἀσφαλὲς λέοντι καὶ προβάτοις ὁμοῦ ποιεῖσθαι τὴν ἔπαυλιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.35.13, compare “IG” 14.1284, etc.}}
**2** **farm-building, country house,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.43, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 24, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus “fragment” 6.1, etc.}}
**3** in military language, **quarters,** ἔ. ποιεῖσθαι en**camp,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.149c; ἐπὶ στρατοπεδείᾳ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.15.5.}}
**4** **unwalled village,** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.25.31, +others}}

1888:
Related to: ** αὐτό-φωρος**, ον,
(φώρ) **self-detected,** ἀμπλακήματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 51.}}
**II** mostly in the phrase ἐπ᾽ αὐτοφώρῳ λαμβάνειν to catch **in the act,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.85, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.132; ἑλεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1214; ἐλέγχειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.42: with _passive_ Verbs, ἐπ᾽ αὐτοφώρῳ ἁλῶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.72; εἰλῆφθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 455, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 181: hence}}
**II.2** in a more general sense, **notoriously, manifestly,** ἐπιβουλεύοντας φανῆναι ἐπ᾽ αὐ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.137; ἐπ᾽ αὐ. αὐτὸν ἐλέγξω 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.30; τὸν θάνατόν τινος ἐπ. αὐ. μηχανωμένη 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.3; ἐπ᾽ αὐ. καταλαμβάνειν τινὰ ἀμαθέστερον ὄντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b, compare “R.” 359c; ἐπ᾽ αὐ. εἴλημμαι πλουσιώτατος ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 3.13; ἀξιῶ σε.. ἐπ᾽. αὐ. ταῦτά μοι ἐπιδεῖξαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.21; ἐπ᾽ αὐ. κλέπται ὄντες ἐξελεγχόμενοι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.10.}}

1890:
** ἐπαφρ-ίζω**,
**foam up** or **on the surface,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus “fragment” 1, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 32.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **foam out,** αἰσχύνας {{NT|NT.Jude.13.}}

1891:
** ἐπαφρόδ-ῑτος**, ον,
(Ἀφροδίτη) **lovely, fascinating, charming,** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.42}}; of things, ἔπη καὶ ἔργα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.15 (_comparative_, codices)}}; ποίησις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.65}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.35}}. _adverb_ -τως, γράφειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 11, compare 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.3.}}
**II** used to translate Sulla's epithet **Felix, favoured by Venus,** i.e. **fortune's favourite** (_metaphorically_ from the dice), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 34, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.97.}}
**III** **gracious,** ἡγεμονία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 77.36 (2nd c.AD).}}

1892:
** ἐπεγ-είρω**,
**awaken, rouse up,** τινά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.431, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 469, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 83}}:—_passive_, **to be roused from sleep, wake up,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, only in _aorist_ forms ἐπέγρετο, ἐπεγρόμενος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.124, 14, 256, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.57; μέχρι ἐπέγρωνται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 1; φεύγετε.. ἄνδρ᾽ ἐπεγειρόμενον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1083 (_anapaest meter_); δόξαι, αἳ ἐρωτήσει ἐπεγερθεῖσαι ἐπιστῆμαι γίγνονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 86a}}: _perfect_ ἐπήγερται is uncertain reading in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Zeux.” 4.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **awaken, excite,** πόλεμον εὕδοντ᾽ {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.19; διωγμόν NT.Act.13.50; τὸ πάλαι κείμενον κακόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 510 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ.. θρῆνον ἐ. [prev. work] 1778 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὅσον ἑσμὸν λόγων ἐπεγείρετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450b}}; **stir up,** τὸ Ἑλληνικόν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139; τὰς ψυχάς NT.Act.14.2; ἡμᾶς εἰς τὴν νεότητα μνήμῃ ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 657d; τοῦ ἐπεγείροντος ὥσπερ μύωπος δεήσει 5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae “Ep.” 1.6:—}}_passive_, ἐπηγέρθη [ἡ Ταλθυβίου μῆνις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.137; ἐπηγείροντο ταῖς ψυχαῖς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.52.}}
**III** **erect, raise,** τὰς ἀκάνθας -ων **erecting** his prickles, like certain fish when irritated, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 1338 (= [5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus] “fragment” 1121); ὅταν ἐπεγερθῶσιν φλύκταιναι 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 17.5.}}

1893:
** ἐπεί**,
_conjunction_, both temporal and causal; also **ἐπειδή**, **ἐπείτε.** OF TIME (ἐπειδή is more frequently in this sense in Prose), **after that, since, when,** from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards:}}
**I** with Ind.,
**I.1** of a definite occurrence in past time, mostly with _aorist_, ἐπεί π᾽ εὔξαντο **after** they had prayed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.458; ἐπειδὴ ἐτελεύτησε Δαρεῖος καὶ κατέστη Ἀρταξέρξης}}**after** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator had died and 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus had succeeded, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.3}}: rarely with _imperfect_, ἐπειδὴ εἱστιώμεθ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1354; ἐ. πόντον εἰσεβάλλομεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 260; ἐ. ἠσθένει Δαρεῖος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.1: with}}_pluperfect_, ἐπειδὴ ἐξηπάτησθε.. **after** you had been deceived.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.42}}; but generally the _aorist_ is found, the _pluperfect_ being used only for special emphasis: with _imperfect_ to express an action not yet complete, ἐπεὶ ὑπηντίαζεν ἡ φάλαγξ καὶ ἡ σάλπιγξ ἐφθέγξατο **after** the phalanx began to advance and the trumpet had sounded, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.27.}}
**I.2** with implied reference to some later time, ἐ. or ἐπειδή, ={ἐξ οὗ}, **from the time when, since,** mostly with _aorist_, πολλὰ πλάγχθη, ἐ. ἔπερσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.2}}; ἐπείτε παρέλαβον τὸν θρόνον, τοῦτο ἐφρόντιζον **ever since** I came to the throne, I had this in mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8. ; ἐπειδήπερ ὑπέστη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; δέκατον μὲν ἔτος τόδ᾽ ἐ... ἦραν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 40: sometimes with}}_present_ (used in _perfect_ sense) and _perfect_, ἐ. δὲ φροῦδός ἐστι στρατός **since** the army is gone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 15}}; ἐπείτε ὑπὸ τῷ Πέρσῃ εἰσί, πεπόνθασι τοιόνδε **ever since** they have been, **now that** they are.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117.}}
**II** with Subj., ἄν being always added in _Attic dialect_ Prose, and ἄν or κε generally in Poetry: ἐπεί with ἄν becomes ἐπήν (so in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1175, “Av.” 983), later ἐπάν (which see)}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἐπεάν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 800 (6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153, +others, ἐπήν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 6, +others, and ἐπειδή with ἄν ἐπειδάν (which see); 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἐπεί κε, ἐπήν (once ἐπεὶ ἄν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.412):}}
**II.1** referring to future time with _future_ apodosis, τέκνα ἄξομεν.. ἐπὴν πτολίεθρον ἕλωμεν **when** we shall have taken the city, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.238; ἐ. κ᾽ ἀπὸ λαὸς ὄληται 11.764. compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.23; ἐπεάν περ ἡμίονοι τέκωσι, τότε τὸ τεῖχος ἁλώσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153}}; ταῦτ᾽, ἐπειδὰν περὶ τοῦ γένους εἴπω, τότ᾽ ἐρῶ I will speak of this, **when** I have spoken.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.29; ἐ. ἂν σύ γε πότμον ἐπίσπῃς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.412}}; χρὴ δέ, ὅταν μὲν τιθῆσθε τοὺς νόμους,.. σκοπεῖν, ἐπειδὰν δὲ θῆσθε, φυλάττειν whenever you are enacting your laws,.. and **after** you have enacted them.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.34.}}
**II.2** of repeated action, with a _present_ apodosis, **whenever, when once,** δαμνᾷ, ἐ. κε λίπῃ ὀστέα θυμός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.221, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.409}}; ἐπειδὰν ἡ ἐκφορὰ ᾖ.. ἄγουσι **whenever** the burial takes place they bring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; ἐπειδὰν κρύψωσι γῇ.. λέγει **when** they have covered them with earth, {{Refs|[prev. passage]: sometimes without ἄν or}} κε in Poets, ἐ. ἂρ βλέφαρ᾽ ἀμφικαλύψῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.86; ἐ. δὴ τόν γε δαμάσσεται.. ὀϊστός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.478, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1225 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1025.}}
**II.3** like {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2, δέκα ἡμερῶν ἐπειδὰν δόξῃ}} within ten days **from** the passing of the resolution, {{Refs|“IG” 12.88.7.}}
**III** with Opt. (without ἄν):
**III.1** referring to future time, ἐπειδὴ πρὸς τὸ φῶς ἔλθοι, ὁρᾶν οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἓν δύνασθαι (i.e. οὐκ οἴει); **after** he had come into the light.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516a: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes uses ἐπήν with _optative_ in same sense as ἐπεί, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.105 (codices), etc.}}
**III.2** more frequently of repeated action, with a past apodosis, ἐ. ζεύξειεν.., δησάσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.14; ἐπειδὴ δέ τι ἐμφάγοιεν, ἀνίσταντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.9; ἐ. πύθοιτο, ἐπῄνει [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.3.55, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d, “Prt.” 315b.}}
**III.3** in indirect speech after past tenses, representing a _subjunctive_ in _direct speech_, αὐτὸς δὲ ἐπεὶ διαβαίης, ἀπιέναι ἔφησθα (the direct form being ἐπὴν διαβῶ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.27, compare 3.5.18, “Cyr.” 1.4.21}}; after _optative_ in a final clause, ἐπορεύοντο, ὅπως ἐπειδὴ γένοιντο ἐπὶ τῷ ποταμῷ.. ἴοιεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80.}}
**III.4** by assimilation to _optative_ in principal clause, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν.. νῦν μὲν ἀνώγοιμι πτολεμίζειν.. ἐπὴν τεισαίμεθα λώβην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.208; ὃς τὸ καταβρόξειεν ἐπεὶ κρητῆρι μιγείη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.222.}}
**III.5** ἐπειδάν with _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in some passages of early authors, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.6 (with _indicative_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.7.8)}}: found in later Gr., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus 2.5, +others, 1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 5.18.10.}}
**IV** with Inf., only in indirect speech, ἐπειδὴ δὲ κατὰ σχολὴν σκέψασθαι, κόπτεσθαι (i.e. ἔφη) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619c, compare “Smp.” 174d, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.10, 7.150.}}
**V** with other words:
**V.1** ἐ. τάχιστα **as soon as,** frequently separated by a word, ἐ. ἦλθε τάχιστα,.. ἀπέδοτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.6; ἐ. δὲ τάχιστα διέβη [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.22; ἐ. θᾶττον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a40; ἐ. εὐθέως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.4; ἐ... αὐτίκα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.35; ἐπειδὴ τάχιστα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.16; ἐπειδὰν τάχιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.9; rarely ἐπειδὴ θᾶττον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.41; ἐπειδὰν θᾶττον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325c.}}
**V.1.b** ἐ. τὰ πρῶτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.420; ἐ. τὸ πρῶτον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1287.}}
**V.2** with emphatic Particles, ἐπεὶ ἄρα **when** then, in continuing a narrative, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.426}}; ἐπεὶ οὖν **when** then, in resuming a narrative, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.57, 3.4; ἐπεὶ ὦν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9; ἐπεὶ γὰρ δή [prev. author] 9.90, etc.}}
**B** CAUSAL (ἐπεί more frequently in this sense in early Prose: ἐπειδή **whereas** is used in preambles of decrees, {{Refs|“IG” 22.103, etc.; ἐπειδήπερ}}**inasmuch as, ** NT.Luke.1.1), **since, seeing that,** frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards:}}
**B.1** with Ind. (after both present and past tenses), ἐ. οὐδὲ ἔοικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.119, compare 153, 278, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.4; ἐπειδή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.80; ἐπειδὴ οὐκ ἐθέλεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c; νίκη δ᾽ ἐπείπερ ἕσπετ᾽, ἐμπέδως μένοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 854}}; frequently with past tenses with ἄν, ἐπεὶ οὔποτ᾽ ἂν στόλον ἐπλεύσατ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1037; ἐπεὶ οὔ κεν ἀνιδρωτί γ᾽ ἐτελέσθη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.228, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.49}}; οὐ γὰρ ἂν σθένοντά γε εἷλέν μ᾽· ἐπεὶ οὐδ᾽ ἂν ὧδ᾽ ἔχοντ᾽ (i.e. εἷλεν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 948}}: especially in the sense, **for otherwise..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.29, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 433, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.14, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.72, etc.: so with}}_future_, ἐξέστω δὲ μηδενὶ.. τεθῆναι.., ἐ. ἀποδώσει.. **otherwise** he shall pay.., {{Refs|“Rev.Et.Anc.” 4.261 (near Smyrna): with _imperative_, ἐ. δίδαξον}} **for** teach me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 352, “OC” 969, compare “OT” 390, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473e}}: with an _interrogative_, ἐ. πῶς ἂν καλέσεια; **for** how would you call him? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 688, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.5, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 214, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 139 (Lyric poetry); ὦ Ἀλκιβιάδη, ἐπειδὴ περὶ τίνος Ἀθηναῖοι διανοοῦνται βουλεύεσθαι, ἀνίστασαι συμβουλεύσω; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.106c.}}
**B.2** with Opt., ἐ. ἂν μάλα τοι σχεδὸν ἔλθοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.304, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 916}}; so after past tenses on the principle of indirect speech, ἐπείπερ ἡγήσαιντο **since** (as they said) they believed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.19.}}
**B.3** with Inf. in indirect speech, ἐ. γιγνώσκειν γε αὐτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 353a, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93.}}
**B.4** in elliptical expressions, ἀδύνατός [εἰμι], ἐ. ἐβουλόμην ἂν οἷός τ᾽ εἶναι I am unable (and yet I am sorry), **for** I should like to have the power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c}}; so εἶμι· ἐ. καὶ ταῦτ᾽ ἂν ἴσως οὐκ ἀηδῶς σου ἤκουον {{Refs|[prev. passage] (here the sense may be given by}}**and yet, although,** compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 333c, 317a, “Ap.” 19e, “Smp.” 187a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1121a19)}}; ἐ. ὅ γε ἀποθανὼν πελάτης τις ἦν ἐμός **and yet (moreover)** the murdered man was my own hired man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4c.}}
**B.4.b** sometimes after a _vocative_, where 'listen' may be supplied, Ἕκτορ, ἐ. με κατ᾽ αἶσαν ἐνείκεσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.59, compare 13.68, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.103, 211.}}
**B.5** with other Particles, ἐ. ἄρα, ἐ. ἂρ δή **since** then, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.185}}; ἐ. γε (ἐπεί.. γε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.352, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 171), more emphatic than ἐ.}} **since** indeed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 181, “Hipp.” 955}}; ἐπειδή γε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 946, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.29; sometimes separated, ἐπειδή.. γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 631, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87c; ἐ. γε δή 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 923, ἐπειδή γε καί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18}}; ἐ. ἦ **since** in truth, ἐ. ἦ πολὺ φέρτερόν ἐστι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.169, compare 156, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.276}}; ἐπείπερ (ἐ... περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.447, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.181) in Trag. and Prose, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 822, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 75, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114d}}; ἐπειδήπερ in Comedy texts and Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 437, 495, “Nu.” 1412, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350e}}; ἐ. τοι **since** surely, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 433; ἐ. νύ τοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.416; ἐ. τοι καί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 677, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567e. [ἐ.}} sometimes begins a verse in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.379, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.13, 8.452, 21.25}}; sometimes coalesces by synizesis with οὐ, οὐδέ, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 446, 948, etc.]}}

1894:
** ἐπεί**,
_conjunction_, both temporal and causal; also **ἐπειδή**, **ἐπείτε.** OF TIME (ἐπειδή is more frequently in this sense in Prose), **after that, since, when,** from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards:}}
**I** with Ind.,
**I.1** of a definite occurrence in past time, mostly with _aorist_, ἐπεί π᾽ εὔξαντο **after** they had prayed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.458; ἐπειδὴ ἐτελεύτησε Δαρεῖος καὶ κατέστη Ἀρταξέρξης}}**after** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator had died and 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus had succeeded, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.3}}: rarely with _imperfect_, ἐπειδὴ εἱστιώμεθ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1354; ἐ. πόντον εἰσεβάλλομεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 260; ἐ. ἠσθένει Δαρεῖος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.1: with}}_pluperfect_, ἐπειδὴ ἐξηπάτησθε.. **after** you had been deceived.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.42}}; but generally the _aorist_ is found, the _pluperfect_ being used only for special emphasis: with _imperfect_ to express an action not yet complete, ἐπεὶ ὑπηντίαζεν ἡ φάλαγξ καὶ ἡ σάλπιγξ ἐφθέγξατο **after** the phalanx began to advance and the trumpet had sounded, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.27.}}
**I.2** with implied reference to some later time, ἐ. or ἐπειδή, ={ἐξ οὗ}, **from the time when, since,** mostly with _aorist_, πολλὰ πλάγχθη, ἐ. ἔπερσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.2}}; ἐπείτε παρέλαβον τὸν θρόνον, τοῦτο ἐφρόντιζον **ever since** I came to the throne, I had this in mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8. ; ἐπειδήπερ ὑπέστη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; δέκατον μὲν ἔτος τόδ᾽ ἐ... ἦραν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 40: sometimes with}}_present_ (used in _perfect_ sense) and _perfect_, ἐ. δὲ φροῦδός ἐστι στρατός **since** the army is gone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 15}}; ἐπείτε ὑπὸ τῷ Πέρσῃ εἰσί, πεπόνθασι τοιόνδε **ever since** they have been, **now that** they are.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117.}}
**II** with Subj., ἄν being always added in _Attic dialect_ Prose, and ἄν or κε generally in Poetry: ἐπεί with ἄν becomes ἐπήν (so in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1175, “Av.” 983), later ἐπάν (which see)}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἐπεάν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 800 (6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153, +others, ἐπήν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 6, +others, and ἐπειδή with ἄν ἐπειδάν (which see); 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἐπεί κε, ἐπήν (once ἐπεὶ ἄν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.412):}}
**II.1** referring to future time with _future_ apodosis, τέκνα ἄξομεν.. ἐπὴν πτολίεθρον ἕλωμεν **when** we shall have taken the city, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.238; ἐ. κ᾽ ἀπὸ λαὸς ὄληται 11.764. compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.23; ἐπεάν περ ἡμίονοι τέκωσι, τότε τὸ τεῖχος ἁλώσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153}}; ταῦτ᾽, ἐπειδὰν περὶ τοῦ γένους εἴπω, τότ᾽ ἐρῶ I will speak of this, **when** I have spoken.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.29; ἐ. ἂν σύ γε πότμον ἐπίσπῃς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.412}}; χρὴ δέ, ὅταν μὲν τιθῆσθε τοὺς νόμους,.. σκοπεῖν, ἐπειδὰν δὲ θῆσθε, φυλάττειν whenever you are enacting your laws,.. and **after** you have enacted them.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.34.}}
**II.2** of repeated action, with a _present_ apodosis, **whenever, when once,** δαμνᾷ, ἐ. κε λίπῃ ὀστέα θυμός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.221, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.409}}; ἐπειδὰν ἡ ἐκφορὰ ᾖ.. ἄγουσι **whenever** the burial takes place they bring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; ἐπειδὰν κρύψωσι γῇ.. λέγει **when** they have covered them with earth, {{Refs|[prev. passage]: sometimes without ἄν or}}κε in Poets, ἐ. ἂρ βλέφαρ᾽ ἀμφικαλύψῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.86; ἐ. δὴ τόν γε δαμάσσεται.. ὀϊστός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.478, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1225 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1025.}}
**II.3** like {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2, δέκα ἡμερῶν ἐπειδὰν δόξῃ}} within ten days **from** the passing of the resolution, {{Refs|“IG” 12.88.7.}}
**III** with Opt. (without ἄν):
**III.1** referring to future time, ἐπειδὴ πρὸς τὸ φῶς ἔλθοι, ὁρᾶν οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἓν δύνασθαι (i.e. οὐκ οἴει); **after** he had come into the light.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516a: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes uses ἐπήν with _optative_ in same sense as ἐπεί, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.105 (codices), etc.}}
**III.2** more frequently of repeated action, with a past apodosis, ἐ. ζεύξειεν.., δησάσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.14; ἐπειδὴ δέ τι ἐμφάγοιεν, ἀνίσταντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.9; ἐ. πύθοιτο, ἐπῄνει [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.3.55, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d, “Prt.” 315b.}}
**III.3** in indirect speech after past tenses, representing a _subjunctive_ in _direct speech_, αὐτὸς δὲ ἐπεὶ διαβαίης, ἀπιέναι ἔφησθα (the direct form being ἐπὴν διαβῶ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.27, compare 3.5.18, “Cyr.” 1.4.21}}; after _optative_ in a final clause, ἐπορεύοντο, ὅπως ἐπειδὴ γένοιντο ἐπὶ τῷ ποταμῷ.. ἴοιεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80.}}
**III.4** by assimilation to _optative_ in principal clause, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν.. νῦν μὲν ἀνώγοιμι πτολεμίζειν.. ἐπὴν τεισαίμεθα λώβην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.208; ὃς τὸ καταβρόξειεν ἐπεὶ κρητῆρι μιγείη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.222.}}
**III.5** ἐπειδάν with _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in some passages of early authors, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.6 (with _indicative_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.7.8)}}: found in later Gr., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus 2.5, +others, 1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 5.18.10.}}
**IV** with Inf., only in indirect speech, ἐπειδὴ δὲ κατὰ σχολὴν σκέψασθαι, κόπτεσθαι (i.e. ἔφη) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619c, compare “Smp.” 174d, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.10, 7.150.}}
**V** with other words:
**V.1** ἐ. τάχιστα **as soon as,** frequently separated by a word, ἐ. ἦλθε τάχιστα,.. ἀπέδοτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.6; ἐ. δὲ τάχιστα διέβη [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.22; ἐ. θᾶττον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a40; ἐ. εὐθέως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.4; ἐ... αὐτίκα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.35; ἐπειδὴ τάχιστα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.16; ἐπειδὰν τάχιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.9; rarely ἐπειδὴ θᾶττον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.41; ἐπειδὰν θᾶττον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325c.}}
**V.1.b** ἐ. τὰ πρῶτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.420; ἐ. τὸ πρῶτον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1287.}}
**V.2** with emphatic Particles, ἐπεὶ ἄρα **when** then, in continuing a narrative, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.426}}; ἐπεὶ οὖν **when** then, in resuming a narrative, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.57, 3.4; ἐπεὶ ὦν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9; ἐπεὶ γὰρ δή [prev. author] 9.90, etc.}}
**B** CAUSAL (ἐπεί more frequently in this sense in early Prose: ἐπειδή **whereas** is used in preambles of decrees, {{Refs|“IG” 22.103, etc.; ἐπειδήπερ}}**inasmuch as, ** NT.Luke.1.1), **since, seeing that,** frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards:}}
**B.1** with Ind. (after both present and past tenses), ἐ. οὐδὲ ἔοικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.119, compare 153, 278, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.4; ἐπειδή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.80; ἐπειδὴ οὐκ ἐθέλεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c; νίκη δ᾽ ἐπείπερ ἕσπετ᾽, ἐμπέδως μένοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 854}}; frequently with past tenses with ἄν, ἐπεὶ οὔποτ᾽ ἂν στόλον ἐπλεύσατ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1037; ἐπεὶ οὔ κεν ἀνιδρωτί γ᾽ ἐτελέσθη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.228, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.49}}; οὐ γὰρ ἂν σθένοντά γε εἷλέν μ᾽· ἐπεὶ οὐδ᾽ ἂν ὧδ᾽ ἔχοντ᾽ (i.e. εἷλεν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 948}}: especially in the sense, **for otherwise..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.29, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 433, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.14, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.72, etc.: so with}}_future_, ἐξέστω δὲ μηδενὶ.. τεθῆναι.., ἐ. ἀποδώσει.. **otherwise** he shall pay.., {{Refs|“Rev.Et.Anc.” 4.261 (near Smyrna): with _imperative_, ἐ. δίδαξον}} **for** teach me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 352, “OC” 969, compare “OT” 390, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473e}}: with an _interrogative_, ἐ. πῶς ἂν καλέσεια; **for** how would you call him? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 688, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.5, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 214, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 139 (Lyric poetry); ὦ Ἀλκιβιάδη, ἐπειδὴ περὶ τίνος Ἀθηναῖοι διανοοῦνται βουλεύεσθαι, ἀνίστασαι συμβουλεύσω; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.106c.}}
**B.2** with Opt., ἐ. ἂν μάλα τοι σχεδὸν ἔλθοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.304, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 916}}; so after past tenses on the principle of indirect speech, ἐπείπερ ἡγήσαιντο **since** (as they said) they believed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.19.}}
**B.3** with Inf. in indirect speech, ἐ. γιγνώσκειν γε αὐτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 353a, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93.}}
**B.4** in elliptical expressions, ἀδύνατός [εἰμι], ἐ. ἐβουλόμην ἂν οἷός τ᾽ εἶναι I am unable (and yet I am sorry), **for** I should like to have the power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c}}; so εἶμι· ἐ. καὶ ταῦτ᾽ ἂν ἴσως οὐκ ἀηδῶς σου ἤκουον {{Refs|[prev. passage] (here the sense may be given by}}**and yet, although,** compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 333c, 317a, “Ap.” 19e, “Smp.” 187a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1121a19)}}; ἐ. ὅ γε ἀποθανὼν πελάτης τις ἦν ἐμός **and yet (moreover)** the murdered man was my own hired man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4c.}}
**B.4.b** sometimes after a _vocative_, where 'listen' may be supplied, Ἕκτορ, ἐ. με κατ᾽ αἶσαν ἐνείκεσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.59, compare 13.68, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.103, 211.}}
**B.5** with other Particles, ἐ. ἄρα, ἐ. ἂρ δή **since** then, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.185}}; ἐ. γε (ἐπεί.. γε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.352, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 171), more emphatic than ἐ.}} **since** indeed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 181, “Hipp.” 955}}; ἐπειδή γε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 946, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.29; sometimes separated, ἐπειδή.. γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 631, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87c; ἐ. γε δή 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 923, ἐπειδή γε καί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18}}; ἐ. ἦ **since** in truth, ἐ. ἦ πολὺ φέρτερόν ἐστι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.169, compare 156, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.276}}; ἐπείπερ (ἐ... περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.447, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.181) in Trag. and Prose, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 822, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 75, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114d}}; ἐπειδήπερ in Comedy texts and Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 437, 495, “Nu.” 1412, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350e}}; ἐ. τοι **since** surely, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 433; ἐ. νύ τοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.416; ἐ. τοι καί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 677, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567e. [ἐ.}} sometimes begins a verse in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.379, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.13, 8.452, 21.25}}; sometimes coalesces by synizesis with οὐ, οὐδέ, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 446, 948, etc.]}}

1895:
Included with: ** ἐπεί**,
_conjunction_, both temporal and causal; also **ἐπειδή**, **ἐπείτε.** OF TIME (ἐπειδή is more frequently in this sense in Prose), **after that, since, when,** from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards:}}
**I** with Ind.,
**I.1** of a definite occurrence in past time, mostly with _aorist_, ἐπεί π᾽ εὔξαντο **after** they had prayed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.458; ἐπειδὴ ἐτελεύτησε Δαρεῖος καὶ κατέστη Ἀρταξέρξης}}**after** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator had died and 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus had succeeded, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.3}}: rarely with _imperfect_, ἐπειδὴ εἱστιώμεθ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1354; ἐ. πόντον εἰσεβάλλομεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 260; ἐ. ἠσθένει Δαρεῖος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.1: with}}_pluperfect_, ἐπειδὴ ἐξηπάτησθε.. **after** you had been deceived.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.42}}; but generally the _aorist_ is found, the _pluperfect_ being used only for special emphasis: with _imperfect_ to express an action not yet complete, ἐπεὶ ὑπηντίαζεν ἡ φάλαγξ καὶ ἡ σάλπιγξ ἐφθέγξατο **after** the phalanx began to advance and the trumpet had sounded, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.27.}}
**I.2** with implied reference to some later time, ἐ. or ἐπειδή, ={ἐξ οὗ}, **from the time when, since,** mostly with _aorist_, πολλὰ πλάγχθη, ἐ. ἔπερσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.2}}; ἐπείτε παρέλαβον τὸν θρόνον, τοῦτο ἐφρόντιζον **ever since** I came to the throne, I had this in mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8. ; ἐπειδήπερ ὑπέστη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; δέκατον μὲν ἔτος τόδ᾽ ἐ... ἦραν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 40: sometimes with}}_present_ (used in _perfect_ sense) and _perfect_, ἐ. δὲ φροῦδός ἐστι στρατός **since** the army is gone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 15}}; ἐπείτε ὑπὸ τῷ Πέρσῃ εἰσί, πεπόνθασι τοιόνδε **ever since** they have been, **now that** they are.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117.}}
**II** with Subj., ἄν being always added in _Attic dialect_ Prose, and ἄν or κε generally in Poetry: ἐπεί with ἄν becomes ἐπήν (so in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1175, “Av.” 983), later ἐπάν (which see)}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἐπεάν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 800 (6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153, +others, ἐπήν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 6, +others, and ἐπειδή with ἄν ἐπειδάν (which see); 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἐπεί κε, ἐπήν (once ἐπεὶ ἄν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.412):}}
**II.1** referring to future time with _future_ apodosis, τέκνα ἄξομεν.. ἐπὴν πτολίεθρον ἕλωμεν **when** we shall have taken the city, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.238; ἐ. κ᾽ ἀπὸ λαὸς ὄληται 11.764. compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.23; ἐπεάν περ ἡμίονοι τέκωσι, τότε τὸ τεῖχος ἁλώσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153}}; ταῦτ᾽, ἐπειδὰν περὶ τοῦ γένους εἴπω, τότ᾽ ἐρῶ I will speak of this, **when** I have spoken.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.29; ἐ. ἂν σύ γε πότμον ἐπίσπῃς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.412}}; χρὴ δέ, ὅταν μὲν τιθῆσθε τοὺς νόμους,.. σκοπεῖν, ἐπειδὰν δὲ θῆσθε, φυλάττειν whenever you are enacting your laws,.. and **after** you have enacted them.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.34.}}
**II.2** of repeated action, with a _present_ apodosis, **whenever, when once,** δαμνᾷ, ἐ. κε λίπῃ ὀστέα θυμός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.221, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.409}}; ἐπειδὰν ἡ ἐκφορὰ ᾖ.. ἄγουσι **whenever** the burial takes place they bring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; ἐπειδὰν κρύψωσι γῇ.. λέγει **when** they have covered them with earth, {{Refs|[prev. passage]: sometimes without ἄν or}} κε in Poets, ἐ. ἂρ βλέφαρ᾽ ἀμφικαλύψῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.86; ἐ. δὴ τόν γε δαμάσσεται.. ὀϊστός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.478, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1225 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1025.}}
**II.3** like {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2, δέκα ἡμερῶν ἐπειδὰν δόξῃ}} within ten days **from** the passing of the resolution, {{Refs|“IG” 12.88.7.}}
**III** with Opt. (without ἄν):
**III.1** referring to future time, ἐπειδὴ πρὸς τὸ φῶς ἔλθοι, ὁρᾶν οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἓν δύνασθαι (i.e. οὐκ οἴει); **after** he had come into the light.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516a: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes uses ἐπήν with _optative_ in same sense as ἐπεί, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.105 (codices), etc.}}
**III.2** more frequently of repeated action, with a past apodosis, ἐ. ζεύξειεν.., δησάσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.14; ἐπειδὴ δέ τι ἐμφάγοιεν, ἀνίσταντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.9; ἐ. πύθοιτο, ἐπῄνει [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.3.55, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d, “Prt.” 315b.}}
**III.3** in indirect speech after past tenses, representing a _subjunctive_ in _direct speech_, αὐτὸς δὲ ἐπεὶ διαβαίης, ἀπιέναι ἔφησθα (the direct form being ἐπὴν διαβῶ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.27, compare 3.5.18, “Cyr.” 1.4.21}}; after _optative_ in a final clause, ἐπορεύοντο, ὅπως ἐπειδὴ γένοιντο ἐπὶ τῷ ποταμῷ.. ἴοιεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80.}}
**III.4** by assimilation to _optative_ in principal clause, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν.. νῦν μὲν ἀνώγοιμι πτολεμίζειν.. ἐπὴν τεισαίμεθα λώβην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.208; ὃς τὸ καταβρόξειεν ἐπεὶ κρητῆρι μιγείη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.222.}}
**III.5** ἐπειδάν with _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in some passages of early authors, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.6 (with _indicative_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.7.8)}}: found in later Gr., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus 2.5, +others, 1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 5.18.10.}}
**IV** with Inf., only in indirect speech, ἐπειδὴ δὲ κατὰ σχολὴν σκέψασθαι, κόπτεσθαι (i.e. ἔφη) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619c, compare “Smp.” 174d, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.10, 7.150.}}
**V** with other words:
**V.1** ἐ. τάχιστα **as soon as,** frequently separated by a word, ἐ. ἦλθε τάχιστα,.. ἀπέδοτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.6; ἐ. δὲ τάχιστα διέβη [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.22; ἐ. θᾶττον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a40; ἐ. εὐθέως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.4; ἐ... αὐτίκα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.35; ἐπειδὴ τάχιστα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.16; ἐπειδὰν τάχιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.9; rarely ἐπειδὴ θᾶττον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.41; ἐπειδὰν θᾶττον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325c.}}
**V.1.b** ἐ. τὰ πρῶτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.420; ἐ. τὸ πρῶτον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1287.}}
**V.2** with emphatic Particles, ἐπεὶ ἄρα **when** then, in continuing a narrative, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.426}}; ἐπεὶ οὖν **when** then, in resuming a narrative, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.57, 3.4; ἐπεὶ ὦν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9; ἐπεὶ γὰρ δή [prev. author] 9.90, etc.}}
**B** CAUSAL (ἐπεί more frequently in this sense in early Prose: ἐπειδή **whereas** is used in preambles of decrees, {{Refs|“IG” 22.103, etc.; ἐπειδήπερ}}**inasmuch as, ** NT.Luke.1.1), **since, seeing that,** frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards:}}
**B.1** with Ind. (after both present and past tenses), ἐ. οὐδὲ ἔοικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.119, compare 153, 278, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.4; ἐπειδή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.80; ἐπειδὴ οὐκ ἐθέλεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c; νίκη δ᾽ ἐπείπερ ἕσπετ᾽, ἐμπέδως μένοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 854}}; frequently with past tenses with ἄν, ἐπεὶ οὔποτ᾽ ἂν στόλον ἐπλεύσατ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1037; ἐπεὶ οὔ κεν ἀνιδρωτί γ᾽ ἐτελέσθη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.228, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.49}}; οὐ γὰρ ἂν σθένοντά γε εἷλέν μ᾽· ἐπεὶ οὐδ᾽ ἂν ὧδ᾽ ἔχοντ᾽ (i.e. εἷλεν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 948}}: especially in the sense, **for otherwise..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.29, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 433, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.14, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.72, etc.: so with}}_future_, ἐξέστω δὲ μηδενὶ.. τεθῆναι.., ἐ. ἀποδώσει.. **otherwise** he shall pay.., {{Refs|“Rev.Et.Anc.” 4.261 (near Smyrna): with _imperative_, ἐ. δίδαξον}} **for** teach me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 352, “OC” 969, compare “OT” 390, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473e}}: with an _interrogative_, ἐ. πῶς ἂν καλέσεια; **for** how would you call him? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 688, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.5, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 214, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 139 (Lyric poetry); ὦ Ἀλκιβιάδη, ἐπειδὴ περὶ τίνος Ἀθηναῖοι διανοοῦνται βουλεύεσθαι, ἀνίστασαι συμβουλεύσω; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.106c.}}
**B.2** with Opt., ἐ. ἂν μάλα τοι σχεδὸν ἔλθοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.304, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 916}}; so after past tenses on the principle of indirect speech, ἐπείπερ ἡγήσαιντο **since** (as they said) they believed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.19.}}
**B.3** with Inf. in indirect speech, ἐ. γιγνώσκειν γε αὐτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 353a, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93.}}
**B.4** in elliptical expressions, ἀδύνατός [εἰμι], ἐ. ἐβουλόμην ἂν οἷός τ᾽ εἶναι I am unable (and yet I am sorry), **for** I should like to have the power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c}}; so εἶμι· ἐ. καὶ ταῦτ᾽ ἂν ἴσως οὐκ ἀηδῶς σου ἤκουον {{Refs|[prev. passage] (here the sense may be given by}}**and yet, although,** compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 333c, 317a, “Ap.” 19e, “Smp.” 187a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1121a19)}}; ἐ. ὅ γε ἀποθανὼν πελάτης τις ἦν ἐμός **and yet (moreover)** the murdered man was my own hired man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4c.}}
**B.4.b** sometimes after a _vocative_, where 'listen' may be supplied, Ἕκτορ, ἐ. με κατ᾽ αἶσαν ἐνείκεσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.59, compare 13.68, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.103, 211.}}
**B.5** with other Particles, ἐ. ἄρα, ἐ. ἂρ δή **since** then, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.185}}; ἐ. γε (ἐπεί.. γε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.352, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 171), more emphatic than ἐ.}} **since** indeed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 181, “Hipp.” 955}}; ἐπειδή γε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 946, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.29; sometimes separated, ἐπειδή.. γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 631, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87c; ἐ. γε δή 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.9, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 923, ἐπειδή γε καί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18}}; ἐ. ἦ **since** in truth, ἐ. ἦ πολὺ φέρτερόν ἐστι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.169, compare 156, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.276}}; ἐπείπερ (ἐ... περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.447, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.181) in Trag. and Prose, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 822, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 75, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114d}}; ἐπειδήπερ in Comedy texts and Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 437, 495, “Nu.” 1412, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350e}}; ἐ. τοι **since** surely, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 433; ἐ. νύ τοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.416; ἐ. τοι καί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 677, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567e. [ἐ.}} sometimes begins a verse in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.379, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.13, 8.452, 21.25}}; sometimes coalesces by synizesis with οὐ, οὐδέ, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 446, 948, etc.]}}

1896:
** ἐπεῖδον**,
_aorist_ 2, _infinitive_ ἐπιδεῖν, with no _present_ in use, ἐφοράω being used instead, **look upon, behold, see,** of evils, κακὰ πόλλ᾽ ἐπιδόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.61}}:—_middle_, ἐπιδόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 646 (Lyric poetry); ἐπιδέσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1414 (_anapaest meter_); ἐπιδώμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 289 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** especially of the gods, **look upon** human affairs, Ζεὺς ἐπίδοι προφρόνως στόλον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1 (_anapaest meter_), compare 145 (Lyric poetry), 1030 (Lyric poetry)}}; νιν Ζεὺς ἐπίδοι κοταίνων {{NT|[prev. author] “Th.” 485 (Lyric poetry); Κύριε, ἔπιδε ἐπὶ τὰς ἀπειλὰς αὐτῶν NT.Act.4.29.}}
**3** **remain seeing,** i.e. **live to see,** τὰ τέκνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.52, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 6.1}}; **experience,** χαλεπά {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.1.13}}: with _participle_ added, μηδ᾽ ἐπίδοιμι τάνδ᾽ ἀστυδρομουμέναν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 220 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ag.” 1539 (Lyric poetry); ἐπιδεῖν ἐρήμην τὴν πόλιν γενομένην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.96; τὴν πατρίδα ἐπιδεῖν δουλεύουσαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.205}}; αὐτὸς λωβηθεὶς καὶ τοὺς αὑτοῦ ἐπιδὼν παῖδας [λωβηθέντας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473c.}}

1898:
** ἐπεισ-ᾰγωγή**, ἡ,
**bringing** **in besides,** ἑτέρων ἰητρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 7}}; especially of a second wife, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.6.2}}; προσώπων ἐ. **introduction of new** characters, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Veterum Censura” 2.10 (_plural_), compare 3.3 (_plural_); κρείττονος ἐλπίδος NT.Heb.7.19.}}
**2** **means of bringing** or **letting in,** ἐπεσαγωγὰς τῶν πολεμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92.}}

1899:
** ἔπειτα**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ **ἔπειτε**(ν) (which see), _adverb_, (ἐπί, εἶτα):
**I** of mere Sequence, without any notion of cause, **thereupon, thereafter, then,** frequently from {{Refs|Hom. downwards, as Il. 1.48, 2.169, etc.}}: when in strong opposition to the former act or state, with past tenses, **thereafter, afterwards;** with future, **hereafter,** ἢ πέφατ᾽ ἢ καὶ ἔ. πεφήσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.140; opposed to αὐτίκα νῦν, 23.551}}; ὃς δ᾽ ἔπειτ᾽ ἔφυ, opposed to ὅστις πάροιθεν ἦν, {{Refs|A. “Ag.” 171 (Lyric poetry)}}: in Hom.frequently with other Advs., αὐτίκ᾽ ἔ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.214; αἶψα, ὦκα ἔ., 24.783, 18.527}}; even ἔνθα.. ἔ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.297; δὴ ἔ. 8.378: usually with reference to a former act, **just then, at the time,** {{Refs|1.106}}; frequently in narrative, πρῶτα μὲν.., αὐτὰρ ἔ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.497; πρῶτον μέν.., followed by ἔ. δέ.., Th. 2.55, Pl. “Ap.” 18a, etc.}}; by ἔ. alone, {{Refs|Th. 1.33, etc.}}; by ἔ. δέ.. ἔ. δέ.. ἔ..., {{Refs|X. “Cyr.” 1.3.14; ἐπεὶ δέ.. ἔ... ἔ... ἔ. δέ, [prev. work] 8.3.24, +others}}; πρὶν μέν.., ἔ. δέ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 724; ἔ. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147c, etc., _falsa lectio_ in{{Refs|Ar. “Th.” 556}}; κἄπειτα, frequently in Trag., {{Refs|S. “Aj.” 61, 305, etc.}}
**I.2** with _Article_, τὸ ἔ. **what follows,** τό τ᾽ ἔ. καὶ τὸ μέλλον καὶ τὸ πρίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 611 (Lyric poetry); τά τε πρῶτα, τά τ᾽ ἔ., ὅσα τ᾽ ἔμελλε τυχεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1265 (Lyric poetry); οἱ ἔ. _future generations,?? 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 672; ὁ ἔ. βίος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116a; εἰς τὸν ἔ. χρόνον [prev. author] “Phlb.” 39e, X. “Cyr.” 1.5.9, “OGI” 90.43 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἡ ἐς τὸ ἔ. δόξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; ἐν τῷ ἔ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67d; ἐκ τοῦ ποτὲ εἰς τὸ ἔ. [prev. author] “Prm.” 152b.}}
**I.3** like{εἶτα}, with a finite Verb after a participle, μειδήσασα δ᾽ ἔ. ἑῷ ἐγκάτθετο κόλπῳ she smiled and **then** placed it in her bosom, {{Refs|Il. 14.223, compare 11.730, etc.}}: frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|A. “Th.” 267, “Eu.” 29, Pl. “Phd.” 82c}}: so frequently when _participle_ and Verb are opposed, marking surprise or the like, **and then, and yet, nevertheless,** τὸ μητρὸς αἷμα.. ἐκχέας πέδοι ἔ. δώματ᾽ οἰκήσει πατρό; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 654, compare 438; χὤταν ἐν κακοῖσί τις ἁλοὺς ἔ. τοῦτο καλλύνειν θέλῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 496; ὅστις ἀνθρώπου φύσιν βλαστὼν ἔ. μὴ κατ᾽ ἄνθρωπον φρονῇ [prev. author] “Aj.” 761; εἰ πτωχὸς ὢν ἔπειτ᾽ ἐν Ἀθηναίοις λέγειν μέλλω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 498, compare “Av.” 29, Pl. “Grg.” 519e, “Prt.” 319d: adversatively, answering μέν, πολλάκις μὲν ὥρμα.., ἔ... διεκωλύετο [prev. author] “R.” 336b; ἔτι μὲν ἐνεχείρησα.., ἔ... [prev. author] “Prt.” 310c, etc.}}; also κἄπειτα after a _participle_, {{Refs|Ar. “Nu.” 624, “Av.” 536; compare εἶτα 1.2.}}
**I.4** in apodosi (never at the beginning of the clause; in {{Refs|Hom. frequently strengthened by other Particles):}}
**I.4.a** after a Temporal _conjunction_, **then, thereafter,** ἐπεὶ δὴ σφαίρῃ πειρήσαντο, ὀρχείσθην δὴ ἔ. when they had done playing at ball, **then** they danced, {{Refs|Od. 8.378}}; after ἐπεί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Il. 16.247; ἐπὴν.. δὴ ἔ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.121}}; ὁπότε, {{Refs|Il. 18.545}}; ὅτε,{{Refs|3.223; ὡς.. ἄρ᾽ ἔ. 10.522; ἦμος.. καὶ τότ᾽ ἔ. 1.478.}}
**I.4.b** after a Conditional _conjunction_, **then surely,** εἰ δ᾽ ἐτεὸν δὴ.. ἀγορεύεις, ἐξ ἄρα δή τοι ἔ. θεοὶ φρένας ὤλεσαν if thou speakest sooth, **then of a surety** have the gods infatuated thee, {{Refs|7.360, compare 10.453, Od. 1.290, etc.}}; so after ἤν, {{Refs|Il. 9.394}}; also when the apodosis takes the form of a question, εἰ μὲν δὴ ἕταρόν γε κελεύετέ μ᾽ αὐτὸν ἑλέσθαι, πῶς ἂν ἔ. Ὀδυσῆος λαθοίμη; how can I **in such a case?** {{Refs|10.243}}; when a condition is implied in _relative_ _pronoun_, ὃν (={εἴ τινα}) μέν κ᾽ ἐπιεικὲς ἀκουέμεν, οὔ τις ἔ. τόν γ᾽ εἴσεται 1.547; ὃν (={εἴ τινα}) δέ κ᾽ ἐγὼν ἀπάνευθε μάχης ἐθέλοντα νοήσω μιμνάζειν, οὔ οἱ ἔ. ἄρκιον ἐσσεῖται 2.392.
**II** of Sequence in thought, i.e. Consequence or Inference, **then, therefore,** ξεῖν᾽, ἐπεὶ ἂρ δὴ ἔ... μενεαίνεις 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.185 {{Refs|cf. Il. 15.49, 18.357; οὐ σύ γ᾽ ἔ. Τυδέος ἔκγονός ἐσσι 5.812}}; rarely at the beginning, ἔπειθ᾽ ἑλοῦ γε θάτερα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 345.
**II.2** in telling a story, νῆσος ἔ. τις ἔστι **now,** there is an island, {{Refs|Od. 4.354, compare 9.116.}}
**II.3** in _Attic dialect_ frequently to introduce emphatic questions, **why then.. ?** ἔ. τοῦ δέε; {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 827, compare “Th.” 188, “Nu.” 226}}; mostly to express surprise, or to sneer, **and so forsooth..? and so really..?** ἔ. οὐκ οἴει φροντίζειν [τοὺς θεοὺς τῶν ἀνθρώπων]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|X. “Mem.” 1.4.11; so κἄπειτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Med.” 1398 (_anapaest meter_), Ar. “Ach.” 126, “Av.” 963, X. “Smp.” 4.2}}; frequently with δῆτα added, ἔ. δῆτα δοῦλος ὢν κόμην ἔχει; {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 911, compare 1217, “Lys.” 985, E. “Alc.” 822.}}
